\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits 10-day high vs euro as U.S. holidays. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Carolyn Cohn \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2002\par 12:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 4 (Reuters) - The dollar rose half a percent to 10-day highs against the euro and also rallied against the yen on Thursday as investors bought back the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tide them over a brief U.S. holiday trading lull. \par \par \uc2  With U.S. markets closed for Independence Day on Thursday and key U.S. jobs data due on Friday, few were willing to take the greenback lower following its recent slide to two-year lows against the euro. \par \par \uc2  "We have seen a significant price move in the euro and given that market liquidity is very thin, a bit of consolidation is on the cards," said Ryan Shea, international economist at Bank One. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading at $0.9761/65 at 0930 GMT, from earlier highs of $0.9750. \par \par \uc2  It was up about a third of a percent on the day against the yen from late New York levels at 120.09/12 yen, well above last week's nine-month low around 118.30 and supported by wariness that Japanese authorities stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quash any export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The euro also eased against the yen to 2-1/2 week lows around 117.20. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce interest rate decisions at 1100 GMT and 1145 GMT respectively on Thursday but both are expected to leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  "Markets are taking steady rates as a done deal," said Shea. \par \par \uc2  EQUITY BOUNCE \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was helped by small gains on U.S. stock markets on Wednesday. The technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index put on 1.65 percent after sinking to five-year lows over the previous two sessions. The Dow rose 0.52 percent after three straight days of falls. \par \par \uc2  "With everyone aware the U.S. is on holiday, we're unlikely to see excessive moves today," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "If anything the current mood of position-squaring will continue and that will benefit the dollar among the majors." \par \par \uc2  But many traders said it was only a matter of time before the greenback met heavy selling pressure again, given that U.S. stock prices have been volatile and were generally on a downtrend. \par \par \uc2  Others said dealers were also unwilling to build new positions with fears over of attacks in the United States on the Fourth of July. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which has stormed up ten cents against the dollar since mid-April, has also suffered in the past few days from European stock market jitters that accounting irregularities like those seen in the United States could spread to Europe. \par \par \uc2  However the poor performance of stocks and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains are expected to help persuade the ECB to leave its key money market rates steady at 3.25 percent for now. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARNINGS \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market several times in May and June to stem a rapid rise in the yen, which it fears could hamper an export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, who has always been a proponent of a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Thursday that excessive moves in the yen could harm the country's recovery. \par \par \uc2  For a lead from fundamental indicators, the market will be looking to U.S. payrolls data on Friday as a key to the trend of the dollar and U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Economists on average expect the report to show non-farm payrolls rose 86,000 in June, while the jobless rate is expected to have risen slightly to 5.9 percent from 5.8 percent in May. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020704dy7400kje\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
