\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar gains as traders cover for Independence Day. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2002\par 09:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 4 (Reuters) - The dollar maintained its path of recovery against the euro and the yen on Thursday as investors who had sold it off to recent lows bought it back to tide them over a brief U.S. holiday trading lull. \par \par \uc2  With U.S. markets closed for Independence Day on Thursday and key U.S. jobs data due on Friday, few were willing to put new bets on the greenback's direction. \par \par \uc2  "With everyone aware the U.S. is on holiday, we're unlikely to see excessive moves today," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  "If anything the current mood of position-squaring will continue and that will benefit the dollar among the majors." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was a third of a percent up against the euro at $0.9770 by 0745 GMT, extending a relief rally from last week's two-year low near parity with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  It was up about half a yen from late New York levels at 120.18 yen, well above last week's nine-month low around 118.30 and supported by wariness that Japanese authorities stand ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quash any export-damaging yen strength. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of England and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will announce interest rate decisions at 1100 GMT and 1145 GMT respectively on Thursday but both are expected to leave rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  DOLLAR SUPPORT \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was helped by small gains on U.S. stock markets on Wednesday. The technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index put on 1.65 percent after sinking to five-year lows over the previous two sessions. The Dow rose 0.52 percent after three straight days of falls. \par \par \uc2  But many traders said it was only a matter of time before the greenback met heavy selling pressure again, given that U.S. stock prices have been volatile and were generally on a downtrend. \par \par \uc2  "It's short-covering ahead of the holiday," said Noriyuki Kato, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING in Tokyo. "We are not in a situation to take long dollar positions." \par \par \uc2  Others said Tokyo players were also unwilling to build new positions with fears over of attacks in the United States on the Fourth of July. \par \par \uc2  The euro, which has stormed up ten cents against the dollar since mid-April, has suffered in the past few days from European stock market jitters that accounting irregularities like those seen in the United States could spread to Europe. \par \par \uc2  However the poor performance of stocks and the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains are expected to help persuade the ECB to leave its key money market rates steady at 3.25 percent for now. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARNINGS \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market several times in May and June to stem a rapid rise in the yen, which it fears could hamper an export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaru Hayami, who has always been a proponent of a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said on Thursday that excessive moves in the yen could harm the country's recovery. \par \par \uc2  For a lead from fundamental indicators, the market will be looking to U.S. payrolls data on Friday as a key to the trend of the dollar and U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Economists on average expect the report to show non-farm payrolls rose 86,000 in June, while the jobless rate is expected to have risen slightly to 5.9 percent from 5.8 percent in May. \par \par \uc2  "I think it won't hurt the dollar's sentiment further if it is within expectations," said ING's Kato. "But if (the jobless rate) goes above six percent, it will start having an effect on equities and so will work against the dollar." \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, weekly U.S. jobless claims showed claims dropping to 382,000 from 393,000 the prior week. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020704dy7400gd0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
