\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Japanese foreign bond buying slumps in June. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoshiko Mori \par \uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2002\par 04:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 4 (Reuters) - Buying of foreign bonds by Japanese investors dived last month in response to the perceived risk of holding foreign assets in the face of a tumbling U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Thursday that net buying of foreign bonds, excluding bills, sank to a preliminary 106.5 billion yen ($888 million) in June from 995.8 billion yen in May and 1,962.8 billion yen in April. \par \par \uc2  Private investors' growing aversion toward foreign assets contrasts sharply with the massive investment abroad by Japanese monetary authorities, which have used proceeds from their dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy mainly U.S. Treasury bonds to offset a surplus in the balance of payment. \par \par \uc2  "In terms of cancelling out (Japan's) balance of payment surplus, it is only natural for official accounts to try to balance out the surplus when the private sector won't," said Masayuki Kichikawa, senior economist at Asahi Life Asset Management Co. \par \par \uc2  To alleviate the upward pressure on the yen as money flows into Japan, monetary authorities were estimated to have spent $18.56 billion in its dollar-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. The government wants to stem the yen's rise because it could pose risks to the fragile, export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has recovered some ground to around 120.12/22 yen early on Thursday compared to a nine-month low of 118.36 yen set on June 28. \par \par \uc2  FOREIGN INVESTMENT IN JAPAN BONDS FALLS \par \par \uc2  Foreign investors' buying in Japanese bonds also fell, to 481.9 billion yen ($4.02 billion) in June from 894.9 billion yen in May. \par \par \uc2  Experts said the incentive to take money abroad has been hampered by tumbling U.S. stock prices, because many global investors have had to offset losses there by selling off assets elsewhere. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stocks edged up on Wednesday as investors did some last-minute buying of technology stocks, but many indices are still at multi-year lows. \par \par \uc2  The technology-laced Nasdqaq Composite Index gained 1.65 percent on Wednesday to end at 1,380.17 after sinking to five-year lows in the past two sessions. The benchmark Standard & Poor's 500 index fell to a 4-1/2-year low on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Foreign investment in Japan's debt market has also been discouraged by a recent rally that drove the benchmark 10-year bond yield to an eight-month low of 1.300 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Since mid-June, the key support level for the 10-year Japanese government bond fell to 1.300 percent from 1.350 percent. The fall in the absolute returns on JGBs must have been a set-back for foreign investors," said Naoki Suzuki, investment analyst at Mizuho Securities. \par \par \uc2  Net buying in Japanese stocks also plunged, to 134.9 billion yen ($1.13 billion) from 931.7 billion in May. Japanese investment in foreign stocks was steady at 646.1 billion yen ($5.39 billion) compared to 599.1 billion yen. ($1=119.92 Yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020704dy740058x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
