\par\par \b \uc2  A Weak Dollar Prompts Multinationals to Hedge \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marcus Walker and Tom Holland \par \uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Juli 2002\par 00:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Staff Reporters of The Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2  The dollar's decline is creating havoc on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  accounting of companies around the globe that do cross-border business. Increasingly, executives are scrambling to protect themselves from further damage. \par \par \uc2  With their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthening rapidly against the dollar this year, many European and Asian exporters have to deal with the unpleasant fact that their goods have become more expensive overseas. The dollar's drop is good news for most U.S. multinationals because it makes their products more globally competitive, but small and midsize U.S. companies aren't necessarily as lucky. Many of these companies, as well as European-company subsidiaries based in the U.S., import raw materials or partially finished goods -- now more expensive when paid for in dollars. \par \par \uc2  European and Asian companies are responding differently, and what's happening in Europe could prove educational for Asia. \par \par \uc2  The low volatility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the past 18 months, and especially the relative stability of the euro against the dollar, bred a complacency among European companies that the euro wouldn't surge and hurt their competitiveness, says Hans Redeker, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at French bank BNP Paribas in London. Many didn't bother to fully protect themselves against such a movement by buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  derivatives, he says. \par \par \uc2  "Now companies are seeing that there is a hedging need," Mr. Redeker says. "But security these days is more expensive than it used to be." \par \par \uc2  Japanese exporters were also caught out by the dollar's rapid slide against the yen. "Our market sense is that they weren't hedged," says Marshall Gittler, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in Tokyo. But unlike a lot of European companies, few Japanese corporate treasurers are interested in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  contracts that would lock them into exchanging their dollars for yen at the current rate of about 120 yen per dollar. They would rather wait in case the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeats its massive dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of recent weeks, which would make the dollars they earn overseas more valuable. \par \par \uc2  That might sound like a risky strategy, but most Japanese exporters have drafted their budgets for this year assuming a dollar-yen exchange rate of 115 yen, Mr. Gittler says. "They are not losing money, they just aren't enjoying windfall profits," he says, acknowledging that managers reported in the latest tankan survey on Japanese business confidence that they expect the yen to weaken -- not strengthen -- from where it is today. In Tokyo, the dollar weakened to 120.40 yen at 5 p.m. Wednesday from its late New York level of 120.16 overnight. \par \par \uc2  For Europeans, where the move to hedge has been greater, euro-dollar options might have been cheap while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were stable. But with the dollar falling, the insurance needed to protect profits earned in dollars when they are converted back to euros has become expensive. \par \par \uc2  At J.P. Morgan Chase & Co. in London, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealing room was filled with the shouts of traders and salespeople as the bank sold options and forward contracts to corporate clients last week. "The speed and size of the movement has definitely caught people by surprise," says Jamie Bonic, the bank's head of European \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales. \par \par \uc2  Most export-oriented companies in Europe appear to be largely hedged in the near term. One example is Natuzzi SpA, a maker of leather sofas based in Santeramo, Italy. Chief Financial Officer Nicola Dell'Edera says the recent volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets doesn't worry him. With 50% of its total sales coming from the U.S., the company pays attention to the euro-dollar rate, and is hedged to varying degrees for the next 12 to 18 months, Mr. Dell'Edera says. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in Asia, there is little exporter interest in selling dollars forward -- that is, to agree now to sell dollars in the future at a fixed rate. "We haven't seen any dramatic scrambling to hedge," says Guido Haller, managing director and head of the regional corporate-risk advisory unit at J.P. Morgan in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  Mr. Haller does report a significant rise in hedging by Taiwan exporters. But customers are choosing more complicated, yet more flexible, option-based hedges over easier-to-manage forwards because of uncertainty over the dollar's future direction. "They don't want to be locked into a set forward rate if the dollar suddenly strengthens again," Mr. Haller says. Options offer the right to buy or sell at a certain price, while forwards lock in a transaction. \par \par \uc2  There has been more dollar-selling interest from local institutions, including Asia's central banks. Caught underweight in euro assets, \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign-reserve managers have been rushing to raise their weightings to the euro as Europe's unified \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has appreciated. In portfolio terms, the shift in weightings is small, says the head of institutional sales at a major regional bank, perhaps only about 1%. But with Asian central banks holding the world's largest concentration of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the absolute amount of Asian cash flowing into euro-denominated assets over the past few weeks has been enormous. \par \par \uc2  One lesson for Asia as its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise against the dollar lies in the differing responses to a weaker dollar in Europe, where many companies are experienced in selling goods and services abroad with a high exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  German business, for example, learned to cope with the strong German mark by relying on lean production, high productivity and the use of imported components, which counteracted the effect of a high mark. German companies also discovered that a sustainably strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brings benefits by drawing capital into the domestic market, deflating domestic demand by pressuring the export-led economy, keeping interest rates low and reducing companies' cost of financing their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But companies from southern members of the 12-nation euro zone -- Italy, Spain, Portugal and Greece -- traditionally maintained their export competitiveness with help from a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The rising euro's impact in the Mediterranean member countries will be limited by the fact that most of their exports go to other euro-zone economies, not to the U.S. \par \par \uc2  That sounds a lot like the worries surrounding Asia today. Despite the fact that a lot more trade stays within Asia than it used to, few write off the potentially damaging effects of a rapidly falling dollar on much of the export-dependent region. \par \par \uc2  -- Craig Karmin, Christopher Rhoads and Daniel Michaels contributed to this article. 