\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar rally loses steam as US stocks back in focus. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juli 2002\par 12:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 3 (Reuters) - The dollar's recovery started to lose steam on Wednesday as a buyback by wrong-footed dollar bears petered out and longer term concerns about the U.S. recovery and asset markets started to reassert themselves. \par \par \uc2  While the greenback held on to positive territory across the board, it slipped from the session highs as U.S. stock futures erased earlier gains and pointed to another week day on Wall Street. \par \par \uc2  "The obvious relationship between the dollar and equities has weakened a little bit because the dollar has overshot above levels compatible with where equities are trading," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Bank of Scotland treasury services. \par \par \uc2  "We had a temporary period of weak correlation but equities futures are turning negative and this coincides with the euro pushing a bit higher. \par \par \uc2  However the threat of further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after it enlisted European and U.S. authorities to act on its behalf last week, remained a deterrent for dollar bears. \par \par \uc2  By 1020 GMT the dollar had pared its earlier gains but was still firmer than late New York levels at 119.95 yen, almost two yen above last week's nine-month low. \par \par \uc2  It was holding a third of a percent up at $0.9828 per euro, at a safe distance from last week's two-year low of $0.9990. \par \par \uc2  Investors have also been keen to cash in profits on the euro ahead of the U.S. Independence Day market holiday. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen over 10 percent this year and nearly hit parity last week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar managed to decouple briefly from U.S. equity market performance this week. It has gained for three days on the trot on the dollar index, while the S&P 500 index hit a four and half year low and the hi-tech Nasdaq clocked up a five-year trough on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  However analysts expect U.S. data on Wednesday to paint a mixed picture on the world's biggest economy, keeping an uncertain outlook about the U.S. recovery. \par \par \uc2  FAILED ATTEMPT \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc came under the spotlight early as rumours swirled in the market that Deutsche Bank might plan to merge with Credit Suisse Group. Both banks declined to comment when talk first emerged late on Tuesday evening. This pushed up the franc its highest for more than a month at 1.4614 per euro, much to the dismay of the Swiss National Bank which has repeatedly expressed concern about franc strength. \par \par \uc2  The SNB lowered its repo rate one basis point to 0.97 percent on Wednesday but the franc held firm against the euro. \par \par \uc2  For Japan, which is concerned that a strengthening yen will damage its tentative export-led recovery, an attempt to contain yen strength has had modest success so far after monetary authorities enlisted the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said Tokyo would continue to watch moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and keep in close contact with overseas monetary authorities to act if necessary. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Reserve Bank of New Zealand raised rates by 25 basis points to bring the official cash rate to 5.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  ACCOUNTING CONTAGION? \par \par \uc2  The eurozone's dominant services sector expanded in June for the sixth month running, but optimism about future business prospects fell sharply. \par \par \uc2  European stock markets have been hit this week by the kind of investor jitters over corporate accounting and company debt which have plagued the U.S. asset markets and the dollar in recent weeks, a factor which analysts say helped take the edge off the euro. \par \par \uc2  The FTSE Europ 300 index was down more than two percent in the European midmorning as the telecoms sector was hit by general accounting concerns. \par \par \uc2  Still, analysts say it is the dollar that is likely to suffer more from a confidence crisis longer term. \par \par \uc2  "Ultimately it will be more of an impact for the U.S. because it seriously relies on foreign flows," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020703dy7300lx7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
