\par\par \b \uc2  Japan Fin Min Shiokawa: Rapid  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves A Global Problem \b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2002\par 02:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Recent excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are a global issue, which is reflected in the fact that Europe and the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen last week, Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "The fall (in the dollar) from Y130-Y131 to Y125 in a span of three, four days, and a further fall below Y120 was excessive for anyone," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  "We can't stand by and watch such excessive movements, so we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  He added that the action by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve on behalf of the Bank of Japan was representative of other countries' views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  seven times since late May in a bid to keep a rising yen from hampering the nascent economic recovery. The most recent action came on Friday, when Tokyo asked the ECB and the Fed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ. \par \par \uc2  Data released by the Finance Ministry Monday showed the government has spent some Y3.3 trillion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  - excluding last Friday - to prop up the dollar, more than the record Y3.2 trillion it spent in seven rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said that there was no change in the ministry's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, and that the government will continue to carefully monitor the market for disruptions, including the possibility of terrorist attacks on July 4, U.S. Independence Day. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister also said that strength in Japanese business sentiment, as reflected in the BOJ's tankan survey on Monday, showed that attitudes of corporate managers were changing. \par \par \uc2  The tankan showed the index measuring sentiment among Japan's large manufacturers improving at a record pace to minus 18 in the latest survey from minus 38 in March. \par \par \uc2  "Like I've always said, when the mindset of corporate managers start to change, the economy will start showing life," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  But the BOJ report also showed that large firms plan to cut capital investment by 6.7% this fiscal year. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said that the lack of new capital investment was due in part to overcapacity at Japan's firms, something which he hoped to be able to help clear through tax reforms. \par \par \uc2  -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020702dy720000r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
