\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar up on short-covering - outlook still gloomy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2002\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 2 (Reuters) - The dollar gained back some strength on short-covering in Tokyo on Tuesday, supported by fears of more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and strong manufacturing U.S. data, but the long-term trend was still gloomy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar briefly broke through 120 yen and firmed to around $0.9820 per euro by late trade as short-holders unwound their positions ahead of the U.S. Independence Day holiday on Thursday and U.S. payroll data due out Friday. \par \par \uc2  The firm tone carried over into Asian trade, where the greenback advanced to 120.00/03 yen by 0558 GMT from 119.65 in late U.S. trade on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $0.9835/40, from $0.9916 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  "The market was trying to test the euro to parity. But after it failed to even reach 99.50 cents yesterday, euro-long holders turned a bit nervous and unwound their positions," a Japanese city bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The market realised that recent euro-buying was overdone and was now consolidating, but there is no change in the basic trend that the dollar is still bearish," he added. \par \par \uc2  FOCUS ON BULLISH DATA \par \par \uc2  Dollar bears have built up short positions due to security fears in the United States near Independence Day, but focus seems to have shifted to the bright signs emerging in the U.S. economy, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  They said dollar sentiment improved after the U.S. Institute for Supply Management reported on Monday its index of manufacturing activity rose to 56.2 in June from 55.7 in May. That was better than the 55.8 expected on average by economists polled by Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The data helped the dollar hold firm in U.S trade despite a fall in share prices - a rare shift of focus for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets that have been obsessed with the health of Wall Street in recent months. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the euro was susceptible to profit-taking after its rapid and almost relentless rise in recent weeks. It gained more than five percent versus the dollar in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  A correction was inevitable with its 14-day relative strength index - an indicator of short-term balance of positions - shooting up to 92 on Monday, well above the 70 mark, which indicates a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is overbought. \par \par \uc2  "Because the euro had risen so rapidly, those who had bought are now selling," said Tomomasa Noguchi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Japanese trading house Mitsubishi Corp. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was also firm against the Swiss franc, sterling and Australian dollar, which all took a beating from profit-taking after hitting multi-month highs last week. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss franc eased to 1.4920/30 franc per dollar from 1.4814 in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell to $0.5606/09 from $0.5624, while sterling edged down to $1.5276/85 from $1.5325. \par \par \uc2  VERBAL THREAT \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary officials continued to talk down the yen after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday, with the Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  entering the market on behalf of the Bank of Japan. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Tuesday that overseas monetary authorities accepted Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through them last week because the dollar's steep decline had become a global issue. \par \par \uc2  "As I have been saying, if the dollar drops from 130-131 yen toward 125 within a span of three, four days and further goes beyond 120, it's a rapid move in anyone's eyes," Shiokawa told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary authorities cannot just idly sit and watch. That's why we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  Separately, Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said the yen may be overvalued, a comment which marginally weighed on the yen, some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar edged above 120 yen, but struggled to extend gains after facing massive sell orders from Japanese exporters rumoured to be lined up around 120.00-120.50 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is rising today, but it should come under pressure again considering there is a huge trade gap between Japan and the United States. As long as this continues, the dollar should eventually come under fresh selling pressure," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0626 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   01 Jul. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9845   0.9916   -0.72    +3.68   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen        119.87   119.65   +0.18    +4.74   114.45\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    117.97   118.66   -0.58    +8.52   108.71\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Dlr/swiss      1.4913   1.4814   +0.67    -6.86   1.6012\par  Stg/dlr        1.5285   1.5325   -0.26    +1.90   1.5000\par  Can/dlr        1.5187   1.5157   +0.20    +1.83   1.4914\par  Aus/dlr        0.5605   0.5624   -0.34    +0.20   0.5594\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/swiss  1.4