\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  officials keep market on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  alert. \b0\par\par\uc2 421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2002\par 05:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 2 (Reuters) - Japanese ministers and government officials kept \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers on high alert on Tuesday for more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying Tokyo's efforts to stem the yen's rapid rise have now won global understanding. \par \par \uc2  A day after U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill confirmed that the Federal Reserve had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behalf, Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said overseas monetary authorities had agreed to cooperate with Tokyo as the dollar's steep decline had become a global issue. \par \par \uc2  "As I have been saying, if the dollar drops from 130-131 yen toward 125 within a span of three, four days and further goes beyond 120, it's a rapid move in anyone's eyes," Shiokawa said. \par \par \uc2  "This was becoming a global market problem - I mean the rapid movement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - and thus I think each of the monetary authorities, although not in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , accepted it," he told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka kept up the usual jawboning, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were a near-term risk for the Japanese economy and that the yen may be overvalued. \par \par \uc2  "We have to watch the trend closely alongside U.S. stocks and the global flow of assets," Takenaka said. \par \par \uc2  The yen slipped on Takenaka's remarks to around 119.81 per dollar against 119.65 in late New York trade. \par \par \uc2  It was still trading at around 119.80 at 0306 GMT. \par \par \uc2  LONE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WON'T WORK \par \par \uc2  Analysts have said lone attempts by Tokyo to cap the yen's export-crimping rise through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could fail as markets remained jittery over the health of the U.S. economy, and sentiment toward the greenback was still bearish. \par \par \uc2  The impact could be limited without cooperation from other monetary authorities, they said. \par \par \uc2  Just when such views were becoming prevalent in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Fed as its agents in overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers took the action to mean the U.S. and European authorities were not opposed to Tokyo's position that a stronger yen could hurt Japan's economy, the world's second-biggest. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi also left traders questioning how soon Japan would act again. "We are keeping in touch with overseas authorities," Mizoguchi told reporters. \par \par \uc2  "There may be more (cooperative \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) or there may not be. But that's only a matter of speculation and I cannot comment on it," he added. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020702dy7200669\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
