\par\par \b \uc2  Shiokawa - dollar fall issue for global authorities. \b0\par\par\uc2 218 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Juli 2002\par 02:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 2 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa said on Tuesday that overseas monetary authorities accepted Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through them last week as the dollar's steep decline had become a global issue. "As I have been saying, if the dollar drops from 130-131 yen toward 125 within a span of three, four days and further goes beyond 120, it's a rapid move in anyone's eyes," Shiokawa told a news conference. "Monetary authorities cannot just idly sit and watch. That's why we \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "This was becoming a global market problem - I mean the rapid movement in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market - and thus I think each of the monetary authorities, although not in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , accepted it." \par \par \uc2  He said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy was unchanged and that he was watching the nervous market with great interest. \par \par \uc2  Japanese monetary authorities on Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve as its agents in overseas markets in a bid to curb the yen's export-crimping rise. \par \par \uc2  The yen was trading at around 119.80 to the dollar in early Tokyo trade on Tuesday, down slightly from nine-month highs near 118.40 set on Friday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020702dy720018k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\