\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: Japan Mood Brighter In Tankan, But Outlook Shaky \b0\par\par\uc2  By William Mallard \par \uc2 1,177 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2002\par 06:06\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Japanese corporate managers grew markedly less gloomy in the second quarter as rebounding overseas economies fired an export-led recovery, a key survey found Monday. But the recently surging yen and falling shares may imperil that improvement. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's quarterly tankan survey of business sentiment marked a record jump in the sentiment index for big manufacturers, to minus 18 from minus 38, offering further confirmation that the economy has pulled out of its worst postwar downturn. \par \par \uc2  The improvement - the first rise in the closely watched index since the fourth quarter of 2000 - was greater than expected. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires had forecast a reading of minus 26 and companies themselves forecast minus 27 in the March tankan. \par \par \uc2  But the negative reading shows pessimists still outnumber optimists. The "diffusion" index measures the percentage of companies saying economic conditions are good minus the percentage saying conditions are bad. \par \par \uc2  The survey highlighted a tale of two diverging economies - Japan's exporters driving the recovery while the mass of domestic-oriented companies continue to struggle under the weight of excess capacity and deflation. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, even the laggards showed improvement. The sentiment index for large non-manufacturers improved six points to minus 16, better than the consensus forecast for minus 20. The index for small non-manufacturers improved to minus 37 from minus 42. \par \par \uc2  But that's a far cry from the record 41-point surge in sentiment among electric machinery makers. \par \par \uc2  And, ominously, much of the improvement in the BOJ survey has likely been undercut already by the yen's recent strength and gloom in global stock markets. \par \par \uc2  "The latest Tankan still reflected some of the euphoria felt in business circles here over Japan's success in avoiding a March financial crisis," said Yasushi Okada, chief economist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo. "The view that Japan's economy was bottoming out fed into that optimism. But sentiment over the past week or so has changed greatly." \par \par \uc2  Pricey Yen Not Priced In \par \par \uc2  The exporter-led turnaround "is a bit more in jeopardy" with the yen's recent surge, said Lehman Brothers economist Matthew Poggi. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been falling in recent months to worrying levels for Japan's monetary authorities. The Finance Ministry began selling yen through the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  May 22 in the first of seven rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at preventing the rising yen from choking the nascent recovery. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen erodes the yen value of Japanese companies' overseas earnings, in addition to inflaming deflationary pressure at home. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  began before companies began returning tankan replies May 30, but the dollar's slump to Friday's nine-month low of Y118.36 probably wasn't captured in the survey, a BOJ official said. The drop below the critical Y120 line "only happened on the last few days of the month, so I would say it would be difficult for that to be reflected in this survey," the official told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Big manufacturers - Japan's healthiest segment - may be in for a nasty exchange-rate surprise. They forecast in the tankan that the dollar would average Y125.73 for the fiscal year through March, higher than the Y124.21 they forecast three months ago and well above current levels. At 0345 GMT Monday, the dollar was trading around Y119.50. \par \par \uc2  For the six months to September, the companies told the BOJ they expect the dollar to average Y126.23 - a significantly more dollar-bullish stance than in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Seven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts in Tokyo, London and New York, in a Dow Jones Newswires survey last week, forecast on average that the dollar would fetch just Y118.60 on July 31, edging up to Y121.40 by Sept. 30. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo shares have also slumped, along with stock markets globally. The benchmark Nikkei 225 Stock Average, as high as 11979.85 May 23, last week was flirting with the critical 10000 line. It ended Monday morning trade at 10613.56. \par \par \uc2  If companies were too optimistic in the tankan about exchange rates, then their forecasts for strong profit growth are also at risk. Big manufacturers, who forecast pretax profits would climb 34.1% for the fiscal year to next March, may have to scale back those expectations, economists warned. \par \par \uc2  "A prolonged bout of yen appreciation will dampen prospects for profit growth, which are currently running very high in the second half" of the fiscal year, said Ryo Hino at J.P. Morgan. \par \par \uc2  In fact, big manufacturers have already trimmed their profit outlook from the 36.8% rise they were expecting three months ago. Overall, companies in Monday's tankan expect profits to increase 16.1% this fiscal year, less than the 22.6% gain they expected in the previous survey. \par \par \uc2  Given the shaky underpinnings for the improvement logged in the survey, economists were also skeptical about the forecasts that sentiment would brighten further this quarter. Big manufacturers expect sentiment to improve seven points to minus 11 in September, while large non-manufacturers forecast a four-point improvement to minus 12. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, the tankan showed business investment, the second-biggest chunk of the economy, is increasingly becoming a drag on growth. \par \par \uc2  Big companies plan to cut capital expenditure by a whopping 6.7% this fiscal year, Monday's tankan showed, compared to the 8.4% cut they forecast in March. \par \par \uc2  But since they also revised downward last fiscal year's spending figures, the planned cut in the June tankan will leave investment lower this fiscal year than they had planned in March. \par \par \uc2  "The downward revision is rather damaging because capex is needed if the recovery is to continue," said Goldman Sachs economist Takuji Okubo. \par \par \uc2  Given such signs of the fragility of Japan's recovery and entrenched deflation, the BOJ is considered virtually certain to maintain its ultra-easy "quantitative easing" policy for the foreseeable future. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has already chopped overnight interest rates essentially to zero, is committed to flooding the financial system with more cash than banks can absorb until consumer prices - down 32 months in a row - clearly stop falling. \par \par \uc2  The tankan found price falls moderating somewhat but deflation continuing to bite. The index for output prices rose seven points to minus 31 for big companies and six points to minus 37 for small firms. A negative number means more companies say prices are falling than rising. \par \par \uc2  Financial markets largely shrugged off the tankan Monday. The Nikkei was roughly flat in the morning session. The dollar was barely changed from Y119.60 late Friday in New York. But Japanese government bond prices fell on profit-taking as the new quarter's trading got under way, with the benchmark 10-year yield up 4 basis points to 1.35% in the morning. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -By William Mallard, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2932;\par billy.mallard@dowjones.com\par    (Douglas Appell, Audrey McAvoy, Miyako Takebe and Arran Scott\par contributed to this report.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020701dy710005u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
