\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL ECONOMY-Factories rev up worldwide driven by exports. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ross Finley \par \uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2002\par 20:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Updates with U.S. data, adds quote, changes byline, dateline previous LONDON) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 1 (Reuters) - Manufacturing around the globe is picking up speed after a long slump but is not yet firing on all cylinders as companies face vigorous competition for export markets, reports released on Monday showed. \par \par \uc2  U.S. industry grew for a fifth straight month in June as solid demand prompted factories to boost production to the highest level in three years, the Institute of Supply Management said, providing more evidence that the hard-hit factory sector's rebound is gaining momentum. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, euro zone manufacturers reported factory output grew a little faster in June, and Japanese companies recorded their biggest bounce in confidence on record, cheered by growing exports. \par \par \uc2  American manufacturers also saw demand from abroad accelerate in June, in part from a roughly 10 percent drop in the value of the dollar against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that made U.S. goods more attractive to foreign customers. \par \par \uc2  "The world trade cycle is still on the up, while the recent decline in the dollar is providing some benefit too," said Ian Morris, chief U.S. economist at HSBC Securities. "It's tough to argue there's anything wrong with manufacturing." \par \par \uc2  ISM said its monthly manufacturing index rose to 56.2 in June - the fastest pace since February 2000 - from 55.7 in May and beating forecasts for a rise to 55.8. Any reading above 50 suggests growth in the sector that makes up about one-sixth of the economy. \par \par \uc2  One example of the recent pickup came from diversified manufacturer 3M Co., which said on Monday its second-quarter earnings will be higher than Wall Street had expected due to higher sales volumes, primarily in the Asia-Pacific region. \par \par \uc2  But Norbert Ore, chair of ISM's manufacturing survey committee, cautioned that manufacturing was not likely to accelerate much further and that employment and capital spending still remained weak. \par \par \uc2  "Production and new orders have driven the PMI to this point. They can't continue to sustain those kinds of high levels," Ore said. "One would suspect we're at or near a peak." \par \par \uc2  AMERICANS ALSO BUYING EUROPEAN \par \par \uc2  The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers' Index showed modest growth in the sector for the third month running after a year-long recession, rising to 51.8 in June from 51.5 in May. \par \par \uc2  The survey of 2,500 companies also showed the manufacturing sector in Germany, the region's dominant economy, growing for the first time since March 2001, albeit modestly. German export orders grew at their fastest pace since December 2000, in part from increased U.S. demand, the survey said. \par \par \uc2  "The upturn is robust and gaining steam," said Volker Nitsch at Bankgesellschaft Berlin. \par \par \uc2  However, the companion survey for Britain recorded the weakest overall activity reading since February and showed the sector barely growing at all. \par \par \uc2  Citing the petering out of a stimulus to the sector from celebrations marking 50 years of Queen Elizabeth's reign and the World Cup, the CIPS/Reuters index for the UK fell back to 50.5 in June from 52.7 in May and a two-and-a-half year high of 53.1 in April. \par \par \uc2  "Firms also indicated being hit by strong competition from the Far East, a lack of new demand from mainland Europe and the erosion of competitive advantage in U.S. dollar markets," the report said. \par \par \uc2  In Asia, the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey showed its index for big manufacturers rose to minus 18 from minus 38 in March. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese are trying to keep their exports competitive and that's reflected in industrial confidence there," said James Carrick at ABN Amro in London, noting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the dollar's slide against the yen JPY=). \par \par \uc2  Unlike the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate the euro's rally unless the market becomes unruly. Instead, economists expect the ECB to use the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength to defer any rises in its benchmark interest rate, now 3.25 percent, possibly for several months. \par \par \uc2  Monday's British manufacturing survey entrenched expectations the Bank of England will keep interest rates on hold at 4.0 percent at least for this month and probably longer. \par \par \uc2  Economists expect the U.S. Federal Reserve to hold its key overnight bank lending rate at a 40-year low of 1.75 percent at least until November to let global equity markets regain their nerve after a succession of corporate accounting scandals. \par \par \uc2  (Additional reporting by Shinichi Kishima in Tokyo, Ruth Pitchford, Ashley Seager, Pratima Desai and Joanne Russell in London and Eric Burroughs in New York). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020701dy7100z17\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
