\par\par \b \uc2  ECB unlikely to use own money to help BOJ cap yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Gould \par \uc2 780 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2002\par 15:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  FRANKFURT, July 1 (Reuters) - The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to tap into its own reserves and join the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite hints by a Japanese finance official that they have already taken a step in this direction. \par \par \uc2  Japan, worried that a rising yen will damage its export industry, on Friday asked the ECB and the U.S. Federal Reserve to sell yen against their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf. \par \par \uc2  But that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which came after the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own on at least six days since late May, was seen more as a token gesture rather than a sign of a concerted move. \par \par \uc2  "It's extremely unlikely to see concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the moment. You'd have to have a complete dollar collapse and that we're not seeing," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose nearly two percent to above 120 yen from 118.36 yen after Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but later slipped back below 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  Japanese Vice Finance Minister Toshiro Muto on Monday declined to comment on the possibility of concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said Friday's yen selling could be taken as a step forward in increased cooperation. \par \par \uc2  An ECB spokesman declined to comment on Muto's remarks. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said the participation of the ECB and Fed in the Bank of Japan's yen-dampening effort was not a sign of a policy change among top central banks, who were not opposed to a gradual decline of the dollar. \par \par \uc2  U.S. financial markets have been rocked by a series accounting scandals at flagship companies such as WorldCom and Xerox and investor confidence has been further hit by worries about the pace of recovery and the war on terror. \par \par \uc2  The concerns have led to a sharp decline in the dollar in recent weeks. The euro has risen by about 12 percent against the dollar since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said U.S. authorities were happy to see a gradual decline of the dollar, which should counteract any deflationary tendencies and give a boost to exporters. Euro zone officials are pleased that the euro is rising, which helps keep inflation under control. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone except the Japanese seems to be happy about the dollar depreciation," said DKW's Dannesboe. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly in recent months to stem the rise of the yen but has done so without asking the Fed and the ECB to act as its agents. Friday's bout of yen selling hit the market when Tokyo was already closed and trading was thin, adding extra punch to the move. \par \par \uc2  Central banks have cooperation agreements allowing them to buy or sell \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in other markets and time zones, giving them the ability to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around the clock. The request is executed almost automatically by partner central banks, which would need strong reasons to refuse a request. \par \par \uc2  Even though the ECB and the Fed were not putting their own money into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , DKW's Dannesboe said Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved the Japanese were intent on slowing down the yen's rise. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese are serious about slowing down the pace of yen appreciation but we don't think they are trying to cause a trend reversal in dollar yen," he said. \par \par \uc2  Klaus Papenbrock, an economist at Deutsche Bank in Frankfurt, said across-the-board dollar weakness was not justified, particularly in the case of the yen. \par \par \uc2  "It is high time for the dollar to weaken against the euro but it should remain firm against the yen, because the fundamental conditions of the Japanese economy are much worse than those in the U.S.," Papenbrock said. \par \par \uc2  But Papenbrock said that other than Muto's remark, there were no signs that the world's major monetary authorities were considering coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Muto declined to comment on coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a news conference in Tokyo on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "On the issue of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I would like to refrain from commenting at this point," Muto told a news conference. \par \par \uc2  Asked if Friday's actions through overseas central banks were a step towards further cooperation among Group of Seven authorities, Muto said: "We did act through the New York Federal Reserve Bank in the way we did. So in that sense, it is possible to take it that way." \par \par \uc2  The yen, which on Friday rose as high as 118.30 to the dollar , slipped as low as 119.79 after the comments before stabilising around 119.65, little changed on the day. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020701dy7100pmm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
