\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar weak as euro parity comes back into view. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Charlotte Cooper \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2002\par 10:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 1 (Reuters) - The dollar remained vulnerable to continued investor disillusionment with U.S. asset markets on Monday, sliding to less than half a cent from parity with the euro. \par \par \uc2  Nervousness the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might order further yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after aggressive action on Friday through the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was the only thing supporting the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Traders were looking to how Wall Street would fare later, after the latest U.S. accounting scandal involving office equipment group Xerox Corp on Friday left investors worried what other accounting irregularities might come out of the woodwork in corporate America. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar is very vulnerable to further crises of confidence in U.S. asset markets," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Bank of Scotland Treasury Services. \par \par \uc2  "People are almost treading water waiting for parity to happen. They're just waiting for it to happen, rather than trying to make it happen." \par \par \uc2  By 0747 GMT the dollar was half a cent off the psychologically important one-to-one level at $0.9950, after hitting a 28-month low of $0.9990 on Friday before recovering later in the U.S. trading session in the wake of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  SURPRISE \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was trading around 119.50 yen, almost unchanged from its post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  close on Friday but more than a yen above last week's nine-month low. \par \par \uc2  "The fact that we saw the Fed and the ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the BOJ was surprising, but again, the impact was limited as the dollar dipped about one yen from the high," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lifted the greenback to 120.40 yen from a nine-month low of 118.36 yen, but resistance proved stiff. \par \par \uc2  Traders were reluctant to chase it significantly below 119.50, seen as a level where the BOJ could step in. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at 118.85 yen up from 118.53 yen. \par \par \uc2  Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said Japan was always in contact with overseas monetary authorities, but he declined to say whether it had any plans for coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on at least seven days in May and June, concerned that a stronger yen would derail an export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources have estimated the BOJ may have bought $18.56 billion in its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June. \par \par \uc2  LITTLE REACTION TO TANKAN \par \par \uc2  The market showed little reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quarterly "tankan" corporate sentiment survey for June, which came in much better than expected. \par \par \uc2  Market players said they were concerned the survey did not fully take into account the recent rise in the yen and the weakness of the domestic stock market. \par \par \uc2  The survey's closely watched diffusion index (DI) for major manufacturers came in at minus 18, an improvement of 20 points from the previous March survey, the biggest gain on record. \par \par \uc2  "The headline figures were impressive. But considering that the recent yen rise and stock falls were not accounted for, I think the actual picture for corporate sentiment should be weaker," Nomura's Toyahara said. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, the market's focus is not on Japan's fundamentals, but on fading confidence in the United States." \par \par \uc2  The performance on Wall Street, above all else, will be the focus in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, dealers said. U.S. stock futures were indicating a mildly negative start. \par \par \uc2  "U.S. stock prices will be more important for the market than economic indicators," a trader at a major Japanese bank said. "Depending on its performance, we may see a change in the recent sell-dollar sentiment." \par \par \uc2  Among key U.S. economic data due out this week is the unemployment rate for June on Friday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020701dy7100g20\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
