\par\par \b \uc2  Japan - always in touch with other  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  authorities. \b0\par\par\uc2 151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Juli 2002\par 03:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, July 1 (Reuters) - Senior Japanese Finance Ministry official Zembei Mizoguchi said on Monday his ministry was always in contact with overseas monetary authorities, but he declined to say whether it had any plans for coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Asked whether concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next move after it entered the market through the U.S. Federal Reserve and the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, Mizoguchi only said: "We are always keeping in touch with other monetary authorities." \par \par \uc2  On the stronger-than-expected results in the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey of business sentiment, Mizoguchi, the head of the ministry's international bureau, said it was up to the markets to decide what to make of them. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was quoted at around 119.61/64 yen at 0142 GMT, slightly above 119.59/69 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020701dy71003pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
