\par\par \b \uc2  COMEX gold drops as central banks  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2002\par 22:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the dollar pulled the rug out from under COMEX gold on Friday, upending early gains and sending futures to a six-week low as bulls turned tail en masse. \par \par \uc2  Much of the capitulation came suddenly before the close, which left August gold at $313.90 an ounce, down $5.70, or 1.8 percent. It managed an equally head-jerking bounce off $310.50, its lowest since May 16, in the final moments. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold fell to $314.00/5.00 from Thursday's $319.00/50 close. Friday's late fix by London bullion dealers was $318.50. \par \par \uc2  Gold was also a victim of investor outflows back into the stock market. The Dow Jones industrial average was up 53 points in the afternoon, despite news that Xerox Corp. would restate five years of results to reclassify more than $6 billion in revenues in the second accounting debacle absorbed by Wall Street this week. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was firm against the yen after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  led the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent a rising yen from choking off \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led recovery. The greenback failed to stay up against the euro, but still ended above the 28-month low hit at $0.9990 per euro in the morning. \par \par \uc2  "We rallied because of the strength of the euro based on the Xerox situation - and there are a few other things that make people feel there's no loss of confidence in U.S. equities due to all this thievery going on," said a floor broker. "Then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drove the dollar higher and that in turn drove gold lower." \par \par \uc2  Gold, equal parts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and commodity over the years, has correlated tightly with the euro this year, reaching 2-1/2 year highs early this month above $330 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  Stock market jitters compounded the interest in gold as a store of value, amid worries about the fragile economic recovery, terrorism and corporate book cooking in the wake of the collapse of Enron Corp. \par \par \uc2  Estimated final volume was a busy 45,000 contracts, almost half of which came in the last few minutes of trade. \par \par \uc2  "It took out $316 with stops below that level and just hammered the market on those stops," said David Meger, analyst at Alaron Trading in Chicago. $310 was the obvious point of the movement but when we said that we weren't expecting to get that in the next three minutes." \par \par \uc2  The long position on the COMEX has been a growing impediment as gold ran out of new buyers. The CFTC Commitments of Traders report released after the close Friday showed the net speculative long on the COMEX contracted to 37,967 contracts as of Tuesday from last week's 41,241 lots. \par \par \uc2  July silver fell 4.3 cents to $4.833 an ounce in a narrow $4.89-$4.825 range. Spot silver was last at $4.82/84, off from $4.86/88 late Thursday. It fixed at $4.87. \par \par \uc2  The net speculative long in silver fell to 39,164 contracts from 44,655 the previous week. \par \par \uc2  NYMEX July platinum slipped $2.70 to $537.30 an ounce. Spot platinum closed at $535/540. \par \par \uc2  September palladium rose $1.80 to $318.80, steadying above Thursday's contract low at $314 an ounce. Spot palladium fetched $316/326. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020628dy6s012wz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
