\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 2-Fed, ECB  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  for BOJ to weaken yen. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Ricci \par \uc2 864 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2002\par 21:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO/NEW YORK, June 28 (Reuters) - Japan enlisted the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the U.S. Federal Reserve to help sell yen on Friday, raising the specter that unease may be growing in the world's three top economies over volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Japan has been eager to dampen the export-damaging strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen for dollars at least six times since May 22. \par \par \uc2  But the yen has continued to gain against the greenback, hitting a nine-month high early on Friday before central banks jumped in unexpectedly. \par \par \uc2  "We are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market," \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda told Reuters. "The ECB and Fed are operating on behalf of the BOJ as well." \par \par \uc2  The dollar immediately surged more than one yen to above 120.30 yen in the New York morning from 118.90 shortly before the action. By late afternoon, it was trading at 119.53. The euro jumped from around 118 yen to around 118.80 and eased to 118.49. \par \par \uc2  Kuroda added: "We will continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets carefully and take appropriate action as needed." \par \par \uc2  An ECB spokesman confirmed it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of Japanese authorities, buying euros against the yen. In London, the Bank of England decline to comment on whether it had taken part in the action, as did the U.S. Treasury and New York Fed. \par \par \uc2  The Swiss National Bank said it had not been involved. \par \par \uc2  Japan last acted to cap yen strength in September 2001, which proved relatively successful as it coincided with a broad-based rally in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But this year the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has soared more than 9 percent against the dollar, largely due to global investors' disenchantment with sinking U.S. asset prices rather than a desire to own yen. \par \par \uc2  Friday's action marked the first time the Fed and the ECB had sold yen for the BOJ during the latest bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In previous instances, the BOJ acted on its own. \par \par \uc2  NOT JUST A YEN PROBLEM? \par \par \uc2  Analysts were divided on whether the joint action signaled that policymakers were worried about the pace of the yen's rise, and by extension, the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  "It seems likely a timely reminder that the dollar's fall had begun jeopardizing the world's recovery," said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York. \par \par \uc2  A falling dollar can weigh on U.S. stocks and bonds by curtailing foreigners appetite for dollar-denominated assets, which in turn makes it more difficult to finance the nation's current account gap as capital inflows moderate. \par \par \uc2  A weaker dollar can also be inflationary. \par \par \uc2  These are problems for the United States. But they can also cause troubles for other countries if U.S. growth slows, lessening American demand for foreign goods and hence dampening growth globally. \par \par \uc2  U.S. leaders repeatedly have indicated they support a strong dollar but have said markets should set rates and have taken no steps to stop the dollar's slide. Indeed Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill has shown a distinct distaste for meddling in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  And some dealers suspect that President George W. Bush might tolerate some dollar weakness to help U.S. exporters. The dollar has risen more than 30 percent on a trade-weighted basis since mid-1995 before starting to slip this year. \par \par \uc2  S&P MMS Senior \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analyst Ron Simpson was one market watcher who said the three-way \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday was not meant to send any signals to the market. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the market is going to get any impression that the Fed wants the dollar higher," he said. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, he said, it would be typical for the Fed to act on behalf of the BOJ or any other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  during U.S. hours. What was atypical was the BOJ's move last month to sell yen for dollars through Tokyo banks' overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desks during U.S. trading hours. \par \par \uc2  It would be far different if the U.S. and Europe had sold their own yen reserves. Such a concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be taken as a powerful signal that the major economic powers were distressed by the action in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  MAYBE THEY'RE JUST BEING SUBTLE \par \par \uc2  But Mike Malpede, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco Group, said the Group of Three heavyweights may have wanted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the fanfare of a concerted action. \par \par \uc2  "They may be trying to avoid the appearance of that in case it failed," he said. \par \par \uc2  'I think they are making an effort to signal to the market that there are concerns from the G8 about the dollar's fall," Malpede added. Leaders of the Group of Eight industrialized countries held a summit in Canada this week. \par \par \uc2  Regardless of what type of signal the G3 was trying to send on Friday, analysts said the BOJ would keep up the pressure. \par \par \uc2  "The Japanese authorities have again displayed their determination to resist a yen appreciation," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Bank of Scotland rates slip back." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020628dy6s00ty0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
