\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar steady but nervous on doubts over US policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 941 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2002\par 05:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 28 (Reuters) - The dollar held steady in Asia on Friday but sentiment remained nervous due to doubts about Washington's commitment to its official strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered around 119.50 yen most of the morning, keeping some distance from its seven-month low of 118.90 hit on Wednesday. As of 0305 GMT, the dollar was quoted at 119.40/50 yen little changed from 119.55/60 yen in late U.S. trade. \par \par \uc2  The euro briefly rose above 99 cents in early trade but fell back to stand at 98.70/75 cents Europe's common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at 118.16/20 yen little changed from late U.S. levels around 118.11 yen. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar was weighed by a widespread view in the market that the Bush administration's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  is different from the Clinton administration's," said Kazunari Kobayashi, managing director at State Street in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  U.S. President George W. Bush inherited a "strong dollar policy" from the Clinton administration but many dealers suspect the Bush team is happy to see a weaker dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Many people think the Bush administration approves of a weaker dollar to help U.S. industries," said Kobayashi. \par \par \uc2  Such a view was reinforced when Bush said on Wednesday that the dollar would seek its level based on market forces. \par \par \uc2  Dealers took his comments as an acceptance of the dollar's fall since April. It has lost more than 10 percent of its value against both the euro and the yen. \par \par \uc2  White House economic advisor Lawrence Lindsey on Thursday said the U.S. supported a strong dollar but added that markets should determine its value. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SCARES? \par \par \uc2  The dollar was supported by expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's strength just as it has done several times over the last two months. \par \par \uc2  But dealers are growingly sceptical about its effectiveness as the dollar has continued to drop even after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The dollar's fall won't be stopped by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if both the U.S. and Europe are welcoming the dollar's fall," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  European officials have repeatedly made clear that they would welcome a rise in the euro. \par \par \uc2  Bundesbank President Ernst Welteke said on Thursday he was not worried by the sharp appreciation in the euro and the fall in U.S. share prices, describing the movements as an expected correction. \par \par \uc2  "We always said that the euro was undervalued against the dollar...now there is a correction not a crisis," Welteke said. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers think the euro's rise will continue - most likely to parity against the dollar in the very near future. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could fall against the yen, too, possibly beyond its recent floor of 118.90, as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone can only slow down, not stop, its decline, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  JAPAN DATA MIXED \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said dollar bulls were disappointed that world leaders did not show any concern about the recent turbulence in financial markets at the G8 summit meeting in Kananaskis, Canada. \par \par \uc2  They also noted that the dollar failed to rise in previous New York session when U.S. share prices posted gains. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial average jumped 149.81 points, or 1.64 percent, to 9,269.92 on Thursday. The Nasdaq Composite Index rose 29.89 points, or 2.09 percent, to 1,459.22. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off a barrage of Japanese economic indicators, which drew a mixed picture of the economy. \par \par \uc2  On the bright side, industrial production rose 3.9 percent in May from April, beating expectations centred on 2.9 percent. \par \par \uc2  But the outlook is cloudy, with the government expecting production to remain flat in June and decline slightly in July. \par \par \uc2  "May figures were pretty strong. Still, at this moment, we cannot be too optimistic," said Mari Iwashita, an economist at Sumitomo Mitsui Bank Corp. \par \par \uc2  Iwashita was worried there could be a repeat of 1993, when a nascent recovery was blocked by the yen's rise and a cool summer. \par \par \uc2  "My intuition tells me things could go wrong. It's been chilly in the past few days and it's said El Nino could appear this year." \par \par \uc2  El Nino is a weather phenomenon - an abnormal warming of waters in the eastern Pacific that distorts wind and rainfall patterns around the world - that causes an abnormally cool summer in Japan, as well as deadly droughts and floods in many parts of the world. \par \par \uc2  Observatories around the globe are on alert for El Nino this year. The U.S. government has warned it could arrive as early as July. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0303 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 27 Jun.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9877   0.9875  