\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Edges Up Against Yen After Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2002\par 23:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A Wall Street Journal Online News Roundup \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar rose against the yen Friday after the Bank of Japan moved to rein in the yen, but it slipped against the euro as the commony \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to flirt with parity against the greenback. \par \par \uc2  In late-afternoon trading, the euro was at 99.15 U.S. cents, higher than 98.84 cents late Thursday in New York. The dollar was trading at 119.53 yen, up from 119.39 yen late Thursday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped across the board after the Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market via the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Federal Reserve Bank of New York in a bid to weaken the yen. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which took place at around 10 a.m. EDT, boosted the dollar by more than a full yen to above 120 yen, while also pushing the euro to just below 99 cents, though subsequently the greenback took a step back. \par \par \uc2  The market action marks the first confirmed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year in which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- acting via the Bank of Japan -- has asked the ECB and the Fed to act on its behalf, suggesting heightened concern about the damage a strengthening yen could have on Japan's fragile economy. Haruhiko Kuroda, Japan's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, confirmed that the Finance Ministry had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The euro has been flirting with parity against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, as concern about U.S. financial markets has grown. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaked at $1.18 shortly after it was launched in January 1999, then slid to its record low of 82.3 U.S. cents in October 2000. It was last above parity in February 2000. \par \par \uc2  Revelations that copier maker Xerox Corp. may have artificially inflated revenue in the 1997-2001 period by as much as $6 billion weakened the dollar Friday. Earlier this week, a similar accounting scandal surrounding WorldCom Inc. pushed the dollar down, although it recovered slightly on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Many economists expect doubts about corporate profits and the strength of the U.S. economy to propel the euro to parity -- one euro to the dollar -- soon. That would be a public-relations boost for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has so far played second fiddle to the dollar as the preferred vehicle for investors and central banks around the world. \par \par \uc2  Overall, the euro has risen about 14% since the latest rally began. The printed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  came into use in the 12 euro-zone countries on Jan. 1. \par \par \uc2  A weaker dollar to the euro makes European vacations and imported goods more expensive for Americans, but would give a welcome boost to U.S. manufacturing exports. The National Association of Manufacturers says U.S. exporters have been laboring under a 30% price disadvantage to foreign competitors because of the strong dollar. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020628dy6s007k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
