\par\par \b \uc2  Risk Aversion Spike Aids  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  With C/A Surpluses \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juni 2002\par 21:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- With risk aversion at the top of their radar screens, many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market professionals are eyeing countries that run current account surpluses and offer attractive interest rates as likely candidates for strongly performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the current environment, with volatility levels considerably higher than earlier in the year, many analysts say \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from countries that run deficits in their current accounts - which captures the balance of trade, the differential between service exports and imports, and the difference between investment flows into and out of a country - will face pressure. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "During these bouts of risk aversion, all deficit or debtor \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par get whacked, that's what took place after the WorldCom announcement,"\par said Pares Upaduyaya, a\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Putnam Investments in Boston.    Events  Friday\par morning only heightened market concern. Fresh revelations about\par accounting shenanigans in the U.S. - this time by Xerox Corp., which\par apparently improperly booked more  than $6 billion in revenue between\par 1997 and 2001 - caused the U.S. dollar first to hit multi-month lows\par versus both the euro and yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But mid-morning \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which asked the\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Federal Reserve to act on its behalf for\par the first time this year, caused the dollar to rocket back, in\par particular against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rising about a yen in seconds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Judging on the activity in the market, I'm guessing some people got\par caught" with bad positions when news of the  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit, said Ed\par Stapleton, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Fortis USA Financial Markets in\par New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Upaduyaya said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from current account surplus areas - in\par particular the euro, yen and Swiss franc - are likely to continue to\par benefit from the spike in risk aversion. "They're going to have a safe\par haven status."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Following the terrorist attacks of September 11, another  time of\par extreme uncertainty, investors moved into Swiss franc- and Japanese\par yen-denominated assets, noted Ashraf Laidi, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst for\par MG Financial in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Other Factors Play Into Investors' Strategies\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But there are other important factors playing into investors' strategy\par when seeking the best plays in times of greater than average risk.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par David Durrant, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer, said\par that while current accounts can play a role, the level of a country's\par interest rate will also be important in  determining where investor\par dollars are likely to flow.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par That's because investors can look to profit either by finding\par attractive interest rates or by means of one \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appreciating\par against another.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Canada, which carries a good current account surplus,  interest\par rates are higher than they are in the U.S., making  its dollar a likely\par candidate for appreciation against the greenback, Durrant said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And there are in fact those who downplay the importance of current\par accounts in times of heightened risk aversion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's a little simplistic to say any country that runs a current\par account surplus will be a safe have in times of risk aversion," said\par David Gilmore, a partner at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While money is currently flowing out of the U.S., which carries a\par notoriously large current account deficit, "The story that we're\par witnessing is kind of the hangover of the 1990s when global capital\par moved aggressively into the U.S.," saturating its market, he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Going forward, less new money will be put to work in the U.S. by\par foreign investors," not in an effort to flee a large current account\par deficit, but rather as part of cyclical progression in global capital\par flows, Gilmore said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2248\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -tyler.lifton@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020628dy6s00cno\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
