\par\par \b \uc2  Dlr holds small gain vs yen on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  chance. \b0\par\par\uc2 260 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2002\par 03:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0050 GMT - Dollar clings to marginal gains against yen as traders expect Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up sagging greenback, as it did on Wednesday. - Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda said Japan will make utmost effort to ensure stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. - But underlying sentiment for dollar still weak due to diminishing investor confidence in the greenback and U.S. assets following latest U.S. corporate accounting scandal and a market perception that U.S. has changed its strong dollar policy. - \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed earlier that Japanese investors turned net sellers of 155.3 billion yen ($1.30 billion) worth of foreign bonds in week ended June 21, compared with their net buying of 237.2 billion yen in previous week. - Dollar at 120.05/10 yen in mid-morning trading compared with 119.98 yen in late U.S. trade. It fell to a nine-month low of 118.90 yen in New York on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2   - Confidence in the dollar was hurt by the laissez-faire stance of Washington perceived by market after President George W. Bush said market forces would set the value of the dollar. - "The American position is unchanged. The strong-dollar policy is the policy of the United States," White House spokesman Ari Fleischer said on Wednesday. - Euro at $0.9827/32 and 118.00/09 yen compared with $0.9816 and 118.00 yen in late New York. It hit a six-month high of 119.48 yen in Tokyo on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020627dy6r0020q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
