\par\par \b \uc2  Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For June 27 \b0\par\par\uc2 1,628 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2002\par 11:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-415-4020. \par \par \uc2  MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME \par \par \uc2  TAIPEI: \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China is holding its quarterly policy meeting Thursday afternoon. Expectations are for a mild easing with at least a 12.5-basis-point cut to discount rate, but economists say any easing isn't likely to effectively boost the economy or slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. (Wrap expected after 0930 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Fitch Ratings has upgraded the long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating of South Korea to A from BBB+ following a strengthening of both the financial and corporate sectors. The rating agency said the upgrade is a testament to South Korea's "unique reform effort" and strong macroeconomic performance. (Wrap expected around 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains poised to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a seventh round of yen-selling since late May, top \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials say. Kuroda expresses satisfaction that global markets avoided turmoil overnight in the wake of the WorldCom accounting scandal, while Mizoguchi says there's no economic reason for the yen's surge. But Mutoh emphasises Tokyo is alone in trying to fight the dollar's decline, saying he's unaware of any plans for concerted G7 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF Kuroda," first take ran at 0116 GMT; "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Mizoguchi," ran at 0121 GMT; "Japan MOF Mutoh," ran at 0053 GMT) \par \par \uc2  WASHINGTON: Japan's G7 partners, long silent on the dollar's slide, may join soon in signaling support for the battered U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Kosuke Nakahira, a former vice finance minister for international affairs. ("=INTERVIEW: Japan Nakahira," first take ran at 0516 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Business sentiment in Japan perked up in the second quarter, a key BOJ survey will likely show Monday, providing more evidence the economy is on the mend. But a resurgent yen and wobbly U.S. recovery have darkened the outlook. ("=PREVIEW: BOJ Tankan To Show Brighter Mood; Clouds Loom," ran at 0413 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =NZ WATCH: Prickly Finance Minister Michael Cullen has sparked a long overdue debate about inflation targeting after having the temerity to suggest former Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Don Brash wasn't infallible. ("=NZ WATCH: Cullen Instigates Long Overdue Monetary Debate," ran at 0358 GMT) \par \par \uc2  CANBERRA: The Australian government plans a review of audit and corporate disclosure rules, a review made all the more relevant by accounting scandals surrounding Enron and WorldCom. ("=WRAP: Australia Reviews Corporate Rules After Worldcom," ran at 0401 GMT) \par \par \uc2  BEIJING: China's Finance Minister Xiang Huaicheng has revised down the 2001 budget deficit to 259.6 billion yuan, which is still a record shortfall despite the better-than-expected result. ("=WRAP: China '01 Budget Deficit Revised Down To CNY259.6B," ran at 0316 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: Widening spreads in Asia's dollar-denominated bond market won't deter Philippine state utility Napocor from raising at least $500 million on the international capital market by August, the head of a state agency working on Napocor's privatization said Thursday. ("=INTERVIEW: Philippines/Napocor," ran at 0744 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: Philippine manufacturing output surged 17% on year in April after shrinking 1.6% the previous month, reflecting a markedly brighter trade picture for that month. ("=WRAP: Philippine Mfg Production Surges In April," ran at 0327 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: Hong Kong's exports and imports fell in May, surprising economists who expected the gradual recovery signaled in recent months to continue. (Data View will run shortly) \par \par \uc2  KARACHI: Muslim Commercial Bank, Pakistan's third largest bank, will issue a 1.6 billion rupee 5.5-year bond in mid-July to boost its capital adequacy in line with new \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  requirements, a financial adviser for the bond said Thursday. ("Pakistan MCB's 5.5-Yr Bond Issue In Mid-July-Official," ran at 0745 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: With financial markets jittery in the wake of the WorldCom scandal, the last thing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders needed was a six-hour breakdown in one of their key sources of price data. Though \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  brokers and Reuters' interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading platforms were fully \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, the shutdown of Reuters price feeds, which are linked to commercial banks' customer order watching systems, caused great confusion for traders of the volatile Australian dollar. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Price Feed Cut Caused Confusion During A$ Drop," ran at 0533 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar hovered narrowly against the yen in Asia Thursday as the dust settled after a bad day for the greenback Wednesday. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," first take ran at 0548 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by 1000 GMT) \par \par \uc2  EQUITIES \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA: Asia Pulp & Paper Co. said Thursday it will fight a recent petition at the Singapore High Court by two of its creditors to put the company under judicial management while it restructures $13.4 billion in debt. Deutsche Bank, a member of APP's creditor steering committee, and BNP Paribas filed the petition earlier this week to ask the court to appoint an independent manager for the ailing pulp and paper company. ("APP/Management," first take ran at 0222 GMT) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA: Canadian life insurer Manulife Financial Corp. reopened its Indonesian offices Thursday, and won the Commercial Court's backing to continue full \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  while it awaits an appeal of a recent controversial bankruptcy ruling. ("=WRAP: Manulife Indonesia Reopens Amid Bankruptcy Appeal," ran at 0854 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: The South Korean government said Thursday it selected a consortium led by Hanwha Group as the prime bidder to buy a controlling stake in state-owned Korea Life Insurance.("Hanwha Grp/Korea Life," first take ran at 0251 GMT) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: Daiei has all but won approval for its three-year reform steps from its major lenders, officials at the parties concerned said Thursday, although talks are continuing with Shinsei Bank. ("Daiei Wins Bk OK For Reforms; Talks With Shinsei Continue," ran at 0244 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: Korea Electric Power said Thursday it delayed selecting a preferred buyer for a possible stake in Powercomm, its telecom cable network unit, to further confirm some of the bidding prices. Kepco said it will complete the sale in July as planned.("Kepco Delays Choosing Preferred Buyer for Powercomm Stake," ran at 0223 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE: Australian miner MIM Holdings Thursday exercised an option to snap up an additional 49% stake in the Ernest Henry copper and gold mine, increasing its stake to 100%. ("=WRAP: Australia's MIM Finally Gets All of Ernest Henry," ran at 0659 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE: Australian-based resources conglomerate BHP Billiton said shareholders at meetings in Melbourne and London voted Wednesday evening to approve the spin-off of the company's BHP Steel unit. ("BHP Billiton/Steel," firs take ran at 0009 GMT; note Correct that ran at 0311 GMT) \par \par \uc2  MANILA: Eduardo Cojuangco, chairman of San Miguel Corp., said he wants to develop the Philippines' biggest food and drink group into a major force in its sector in Southeast Asia. The group announced its five-month results Thursday, showing consolidated sales rose 28% and operating income grew 18% on year. (Wrap expected around 1030 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE: Singapore Telecommunications, which is listed in Singapore and Australia, said Thursday it doesn't plan to change its dual listing status. ("Singapore Telecom Reaffirms Plans To Keep Dual Listing," ran at 0849 GMT) \par \par \uc2  SEOUL: LG Electronics said Thursday it will invest $90 million by 2005 in its Mexico plant to expand digital television output. ("S Korea LG Elec To Invest $90M In Mexico Plant By 2005," ran at 0105 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIA FUND POLL: International fund managers are still bullish on Asian equities despite the markets' slide in recent weeks, the latest monthly Dow Jones poll of fund managers shows. The survey was taken just before the market volatility caused by Wednesday's news of the WorldCom accounting scandal, but many managers said that didn't change their recommendations. ("=Asia Fund Poll: Still Bullish On Regional Equities," ran at 0422 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIA FUND VIEW: Malaysia's politically inspired stock selloff this week provides an "unequivocally compelling opportunity" to buy into the market at current valuations, Merrill Lynch said Thursday. ("=Asia Fund View: Merrill Lynch Recommends Malaysian Stks," ran at 0326 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Even in the normally turbulent Chinese stock markets, French retailer Carrefour's joint-venture plans have been making waves. After the supermarket operator said in early June it would sell stakes in existing and future stores to local companies to comply with Chinese regulations, local investors have been snapping up shares in the domestic retailers they believe will benefit. ("=Asian Stock Focus: China Retailers Up On Merger, JV Talk," ran at 0539 GMT) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG: ABN Amro Bank and Eureka Hedge Advisors launched Thursday a hedge fund index aimed at providing a benchmark for measuring the performance of such alternative products that invest in Asia. The ABN Amro EurekaHedge Index is the second benchmark for Asian hedge funds to be launched so far. ("ABN Amro/Eureka/Index," first take ran at 0626 GMT) \par \par \uc2  =TECHICAL TALK: The Straits Times Index is gaining Thursday after hitting a near seven-month closing low Wednesday, but technical charts suggest it may be too early to bring out the champagne. ("=TECHNICAL TALK: STI Downtrend Slowing But Not Over Yet," ran at 0738 GMT) \par \par \uc2  ENERGY/COMMODITIES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO: The unfolding of yet another accounting scandal at a major U.S. corporation has knocked the dollar sharply lower and hastened the exodus of funds from U.S. equities markets. But the WorldCom accounting debacle has put a much-needed gold price correction on hold. ("=INTERVIEW: WorldCom Scandal Delays Gold Price Correction," ran at 0333 GMT) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020627dy6r000ko\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
