\par\par \b \uc2  PREVIEW: BOJ Tankan To Show Brighter Mood; Clouds Loom \b0\par\par\uc2  By Arran Scott \par \uc2 1,048 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2002\par 06:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Business sentiment in Japan perked up in the second quarter, a key survey will likely show Monday, providing more evidence the economy is on the mend. But a resurgent yen and wobbly U.S. recovery have darkened the outlook. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan's June tankan is expected to show sentiment improved for the first time in a year and a half, reflecting relief among corporate managers Japan's economy might be past its nadir, as stronger exports fuel a smart rebound in manufacturing. \par \par \uc2  The closely watched sentiment index for big manufacturers is expected to improve to minus 26 from minus 38 in the March survey, marking the first improvement since the fourth quarter of 2000, according to a poll of 28 economists by Dow Jones Newswires. That is a tad better than the minus 27 reading the companies themselves forecast in the previous tankan. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ is scheduled to released the survey Monday at 8:50 a.m., which is Sunday at 2350 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The brighter mood would confirm Japan is pulling out its worst recession in the postwar period. But the survey will also likely show most domestic-oriented companies continue to struggle under the weight of excess capacity, deflation and flagging demand. That means the tankan won't sway the BOJ in its flooding the economy will liquidity, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The large non-manufacturers index likely improved only a marginal two points to minus 20, underscoring the bitter domestic conditions. \par \par \uc2  And the negative readings would indicate pessimists still outnumber optimists by a mile. The tankan's "diffusion" indexes measure the percentage of companies saying economic conditions are good minus the percentage saying conditions are bad; the indexes ignore fence-sitters. \par \par \uc2  "I think the tankan will remind the market that Japan's economy is making progress toward recovery, step by step," said Takuji Okubo, an economist at Goldman Sachs. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts, however, warn that the relatively upbeat tankan might not provide a reliable indicator of Japan's prospects. Much of the recent optimism stems from hopes that rebounding demand overseas and a weak yen would enable Japan's export-dependent manufacturers to keep revving up production. But the basis for optimism might be starting to crumble. \par \par \uc2  A broad-based selloff in the dollar amid growing worries about the health of the U.S. economy and evaporating faith in U.S. companies' books have caused the yen to soar, despite rounds of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid to a nine-month low of Y118.90 overnight, though its has since rebounded slightly. Thursday at 0355 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at Y120.15 - still well below Y124.21, the average rate for this fiscal year that companies forecast in the March tankan. \par \par \uc2  A stronger yen squeezes profits of Japan's exporters and adds to the deflationary pressure already squelching debt-burdened companies out of existence near record numbers. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stock prices have also been walloped in the last month, taking their biggest hit Wednesday on news of what may be the biggest corporate accounting fraud in history by WorldCom Inc., as well as the strong yen. The Nikkei 225 Stock Average sank 4.0% 10074.56, its lowest since Feb. 20 and down 16% from its late May high for the year. The Nikkei rebounded 2.0% Thursday morning. \par \par \uc2  Still, Monday's tankan will likely show companies expect a sharp recovery in profits for the fiscal year to March - a boon for corporate Japan, which got hammered last year by the rout in the IT sector and slump in foreign demand. \par \par \uc2  With inventories at their lowest level in since 1989 and shipments picking up, manufacturers can looking forward to swinging well into the black. Japanese companies typically have huge fixed costs and any rise in sales tends to feed directly into their bottom lines. \par \par \uc2  Daiwa Institute of Research forecasts profits at 300 of Japan's largest listed companies will soar 57% this fiscal year - a dramatic turnaround from last fiscal year's 39.4% plunge. \par \par \uc2  The problem, though, is many companies are still swimming in excess capacity and loaded with debt. That means managers have little incentive to invest in new plant and equipment and are instead using the extra cash to pay back loans. \par \par \uc2  "Firms are still in restructuring mode in which they want to reduce their total capacity," said Matthew Poggi, an economist at Lehman Brothers. \par \par \uc2  The tankan is expected to show big companies on average plan to cut capital expenditure by 7.2% this fiscal year. That would be slightly less than the 8.4% reduction companies said they planned in the March tankan. \par \par \uc2  But the investment cutbacks are a clear indication that Japan's economy isn't home free. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                           Large Co Sentiment  FY Capex Plans\par                               Mfrs  Non-Mfrs   Large Cos\par Okasan Research                 -30   -20       -8.8\par Dai-Ichi Life Research          -24   -16       -6.8\par Goldman Sachs                   -26   -20       -6.9\par Merrill Lynch Sec               -27   -21        N/A\par Sakura Investment               -30   -20       -7.8\par UBS Warburg                     -20   -19       -7.2\par Sanwa Research                  -27   -19       -8.3\par Shinkin \f28 \b \hig