\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Outlook: Euro To Hit Parity In July, Stay Above $1 \b0\par\par\uc2  By Kristyne E. Demske \par \uc2 1,148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2002\par 21:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  OF DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Continuing dollar weakness will see the euro hit parity and beyond by the end of July, according to most of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts responding to a Dow Jones Newswires survey. \par \par \uc2  But at least some analysts believe the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be able to hold onto its short-term gains. Analysts' opinions are divided on the dollar's near-term prospects versus the yen, but most see the greenback recovering some ground against its Japanese counterpart by the end of September. \par \par \uc2  Investors are focusing on the dismal outlook for U.S. equities and this will continue to drag the dollar down, said Minoru Shioiri, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Kokusai Securities in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will likely hit parity against the dollar as early as next week," said Shioiri. "With the outlook for the dollar weak, European officials welcoming the euro's rise and no fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, it's only a matter of time before the euro reaches parity." \par \par \uc2  Shioiri predicted the euro would keep going and reach $1.0200 by the end of July, but return to $1.0000 by the end  of September. \par \par \uc2  At 3:00 p.m. EDT (1900 GMT) Thursday, the euro was at $0.9853, up from $0.9832 earlier in London and $0.9815 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was trading at Y119.53, down from Y119.89 earlier in London and Y119.97 late Wednesday in New York. In relation to the yen, euro was at Y117.78, up from Y117.68 Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  London analysts also say stock market woes, namely poor corporate profitability and continuing accounting scandals, will depress the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The euro will definitely break through parity, said T.J.  Marta, senior analyst at Citibank in London, because of hurting U.S. equities and an interest-rate yield disadvantage that has reduced incentive for foreign investors to move into the dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said there hasn't been consistently strong U.S. economic data to lead the Federal Reserve to raise interest rates. Higher U.S. rates would bring in more foreign investors and support the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The euro will likely hit parity toward the end of July, and will likely fluctuate after clearing $1.0000," said Masamichi Koike, senior vice president and head of spot  \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in Tokyo. "But it will likely settle back down near $1.0100  at the end of September." \par \par \uc2  Parity is psychologically a very important number, but once it hits this level, it will most likely come back down again because it isn't the strength of the euro that is driving the dollar's fall, it's the overall weakness of the dollar, said Ashraf Laidi, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at MG Financial Group. \par \par \uc2  And the dollar won't stay weak indefinitely because current levels are not generlly favorable to the economies of the countries in Asia and Europe that are maintaining the  stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Laidi. The leaders of the Group of Eight most industrialized countries will soon realize that the accelerated fall of the dollar won't be in their interest, or in the interest of the United States. \par \par \uc2  "They cannot afford to see the dollar falling so much, so fast," he said. "If it falls so much, that means it's going to drive up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and if it drives their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up, it's going to crimp their exports." \par \par \uc2  The euro has risen too far, too fast, which will temper its near-term increases, said Marcel Kasumovich, an economist at Goldman Sachs in New York. \par \par \uc2  "What we're seeing, in part, is an overshooting in the path toward a longer term readjustment in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he  said. Kasumovich said by the end of September, the euro would be back down to $0.9300. \par \par \uc2  A majority of the analysts agree the yen will head downward, giving the dollar a little relief as the focus turns to Asia. Koike, at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in Tokyo, said in the near-term, the dollar will still see weakness, but "as the end of Japan's Sept. 30 fiscal first-half approaches, the focus will likely return to more Japan-negative factors, such as poor corporate earnings, sending the yen back down." \par \par \uc2  Koike said he sees the dollar at Y118.50 at the end of July and all the way up to Y124.00 at the end of September.   Some analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar hits key levels. \par \par \uc2  But Laidi said further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MOF will probably not be used in the manner which it had in the past.  Instead of trying to keep the dollar above Y125 or Y126, they would be trying to halt any further appreciation of the  yen. \par \par \uc2  However, Laidi also noted that if this is the MOF's intention, it had better make it known soon so that the dollar doesn't continue a severe downward trend which would be harmful to Japanese exporters who rely on a weak yen. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "They really need to get their act together," he said.  "If they don't\par step up their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , they could see the dollar hit Y114, Y115\par levels."\par    Table of One-Month and Three-Month Forecasts\par                    July 31         Sept. 30\par    T.J. Marta, senior analyst at Citibank in New York\par    euro/dollar     $1.0000         $1.0200\par    euro/yen        Y119.50         Y123.00\par    dollar/yen      Y120.00         Y121.00\par    Marcel Kasumovich, economist at Goldman Sachs in\par    New York\par    euro/dollar     N/A             $0.9300\par    euro/yen        N/A             Y122.80\par    dollar/yen      N/A             Y132.00\par    Ashraf Laidi, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at MG\par    Financial Group in New York\par    euro/dollar     $0.9850         $0.9600\par    euro/yen        Y124.00         Y120.00\par    dollar/yen      Y117.00         Y121.00\par    Steve Barrow, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bear\par    Stearns in London\par    euro/dollar     $1.0000         $1.0200\par    euro/yen        Y119.00         Y122.00\par    dollar/yen      Y119.00         Y120.00\par    Simon Derrick, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank\par    of New York in London\par    euro/dollar     $1.0000         $1.0300\par    euro/yen        Y118.00         Y119.50\par    dollar/yen      Y118.00         Y116.00\par    Minoru Shioiri, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at\par    Kokusai Securities in Tokyo\par    euro/dollar     $1.0200         $1.0000\par    euro/yen        Y121.00         Y116.00\par    dollar/yen      Y119.00         Y116.00\par    Masamichi Koike, senior vice president, head of\par    spot \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading at Sumitomo Mitsui\par    Banking