\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2  to curb yen's rise vs dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Yoko Nishikawa \par \uc2 716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2002\par 12:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 26 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Wednesday for the second time this week in a bid to curb a steep rise in the yen, which it fears could choke off its economy's export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  As the yen rose towards the psychologically crucial level of 120 yen to the dollar, the Bank of Japan stepped into the market on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which immediately confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We took appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today just as the day before yesterday," Haruhiko Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  The yen retreated as low as 121.35 yen after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The MOF said it followed through with a second round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the yen began clawing back up. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers characterised the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as heavy, although no specific figure was immediately available for the amount sold by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Kuroda and other MOF officials have been warning almost on a daily basis that volatility in exchange rates is undesirable and that they would take "appropriate action" if needed. \par \par \uc2  Still, the action came as a surprise to some traders, who had expected the government to keep a low profile during the two-day Group of Eight summit meeting in Canada which began on Wednesday, given the political sensitivity of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues. \par \par \uc2  President George W. Bush's administration is facing political pressure from domestic manufacturers to abandon its "strong dollar" policy, but at the same time concern is growing that a weaker dollar could halt international investment flows into the United States which are needed to fund its yawning current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials have also warned Japan against relying on a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports instead of squarely addressing the deep-rooted problems in its economy. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill declined to talk about the dollar but said Japan's floundering economy was a "real problem", reflecting concern that Japan's weakness remained a drag on world growth. \par \par \uc2  "Japan is in a real problem and it's not clear that they will be able to get out given their inflexibility," O'Neill said during questioning after a speech at a U.S. university. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials sought to justify their market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the ruling Liberal Democratic Party's policy council chief, Taro Aso, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to counter rapid market gyrations was acceptable. \par \par \uc2  EXPORTING AWAY TROUBLES \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, government data showed that retail sales fell in May from year-ago levels for the 14th straight month, underlining views that Japan's export-led recovery has yet to cheer consumers and remains hostage to swings in global demand and a surging yen. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen about 10 percent against the dollar since April 1 - a worry for the government, which has spent an estimated $25 billion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets trying to hold it down in the past few weeks. \par \par \uc2  A non-committal policy stance by the U.S. government on exchange rates has also been a factor behind the dollar's broad-based decline in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, the dollar also fell to 28-month lows around $0.9880 against the euro, edging toward parity of $1.00 with the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar came under renewed pressure after President George W. Bush, in Kananaskis, Canada, for the G8 summit, said the dollar would seek its level based on market forces and the performance of the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also undermined by concerns about a stalling U.S. economic recovery and shaky Wall Street stocks. \par \par \uc2  A fresh blow to sentiment came on Tuesday after news that WorldCom Inc, the No. 2 U.S. long-distance carrier, fired its chief financial officer after uncovering improper accounting of almost $4 billion in expenses, in the latest financial scandal to rock Corporate America. \par \par \uc2  "The news of WorldCom and a U.S. equity market decline and the remarks by Bush and O'Neill all added to the bearish sentiment for the dollar," said Minori Takeuchi, vice president at the Research Group of the Global Trading Division of Chase Manhattan Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020626dy6q00lau\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
