\par\par \b \uc2  Japan tries and fails to halt dollar slide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chikafumi Hodo \par \uc2 958 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2002\par 09:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 26 (Reuters) - Worried Japanese authorities waded in to buy dollars for the second time this week on Wednesday, but failed to halt convincingly the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide in the wake of America's latest corporate scandal. \par \par \uc2  Heavy buying by the Bank of Japan was barely enough to offset the prospect of a major fall-out on U.S. asset markets from U.S. telecoms giant WorldCom's admission that it had inflated its profits. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was briefly lifted by the BOJ but with Washinghton apparently unfazed by its weakness the effect was short-lived and heavy selling put a lid on the struggling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 121.50 yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar had earlier slumped to a 7-= month low of 120.16 yen and a 28-month low of $0.9880 per euro as nervousness over U.S. stocks intensified after the WorldCom shock. \par \par \uc2  As of 0646 GMT, the dollar had given up most \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains and drifted down to 120.20/26 yen against Tuesday's late U.S. level of 121.34/42 yen. The euro was firm at $0.9933/38 against $0.9790/95. \par \par \uc2  The latest bout of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , concerned that a stronger yen will derail its export-led recovery, came on top of the more than 3 trillion yen ($24.77 billion) it has spent buying dollars since late May. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, who acts as the agent of the Finance Ministry, was detected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times after initially buying dollars around 120.20-120.30 yen in the afternoon in what dealers characterised as heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top financial diplomat, Haruhiko Kuroda, confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "We took appropriate action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today just as the day before yesterday," Kuroda, vice finance minister for international affairs, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  WORLDCOM REVELATION \par \par \uc2  The WorldCom revelation is seen as the latest example of accounting irregularities in corporate America following the Enron debacle and is set to raise more doubts over U.S. assets. \par \par \uc2  Nasdaq September futures and S&P 500 futures were down sharply in Asian trade, boding ill for U.S. markets when they get their first chance to react later on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  At 0638 GMT, September Nasdaq futures traded on Globex had dropped 50 points to 977.00. September S&P 500 futures had fallen 28.50 points to 946.00. \par \par \uc2  "The effect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be limited because the market is selling the dollar on the heightening worries about the U.S. stock market or the potential fears about the U.S. economy itself," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the interbank desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank. \par \par \uc2  "This problem of WorldCom could depress U.S. share market even further, as well as the dollar," Furumaya said. "We want to see how U.S. share markets react later in the day. \par \par \uc2  For their part, U.S. leaders are doing nothing to support the Japanese campaign to halt the dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar will seek its level based upon market forces, and based on whether or not our country can rein in spending, can recover, can revitalise our manufacturing base," U.S. President George W. Bush said on Tuesday from the G8 summit in Canada. \par \par \uc2  BUSH RELAXED \par \par \uc2  His remark hit early Tokyo trading on Wednesday, deepening the dollar's losses against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was worried about building new dollar positions amid Washington's laissez-faire stance towards the tumbling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and deteriorating share markets. \par \par \uc2  "The news of WorldCom and a U.S. equity market decline and the remarks by Bush and O'Neill all added to the bearish sentiment for the dollar," said Minori Takeuchi, vice president at the Research Group of the Global Trading Division of Chase Manhattan Bank in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill simply said on Tuesday that the market was delivering its own judgment on the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "There is no sense of urgency in Bush's remark," Takeuchi said. \par \par \uc2  She added that the laissez-faire stance could backfire on the U.S. because a weaker dollar would keep Latin American shares under pressure, which in turn hurts U.S. equity markets and prompts more outflows of capital from U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  Latin American stocks sagged in U.S. trading. The Bank of New York's index of leading Latin American ADRs (American Depositary Receipts) tumbled 1.1 percent, while its index of Brazilian ADRs fell 1.18 percent. \par \par \uc2  ($1=121.13 Yen) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0614 GMT. All data taken from Reuters with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 GMT. \par \par \uc2   Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls \par \par \uc2   25 Jun. \par \par \uc2   ---------------------------------------- \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/dlr       0.9873   0.9795   +0.80    +3.97   0.9496\par  Dlr/yen        120.54   121.31   -0.63    +5.32   114.45\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Euro/yen    118.99   118.89   +0.08    +9.46   108.71\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  D