\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: WorldCom Debacle Shakes Asian Markets \b0\par\par\uc2  By Brian Fowler \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 1,073 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2002\par 10:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian financial markets shuddered Wednesday in response to news of what may be one of the largest accounting frauds in history at U.S. telecom giant WorldCom Inc. \par \par \uc2  The news broke as trading began in several Asian countries, sending almost all regional stock markets plunging. Domestic bond prices climbed as investors sought safe-haven assets, and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose against the U.S. dollar on the prospect of more capital flight from the U.S. \par \par \uc2  WorldCom announced $3.8 billion of expenses were improperly booked as capital expenditures, obliging it to restate its earnings. Analysts said that could force bankruptcy for the firm, which has been hobbled by weak demand for its services, a probe by the Securities and Exchange Commission and $30 billion in debt. \par \par \uc2  Following last year's collapse of Enron Corp., the WorldCom news dealt another heavy blow to faith in U.S. asset markets, worsening concern about widespread accounting abuses in Corporate America. \par \par \uc2  Worldcom's announcement also coincided with weak June consumer confidence data from the U.S., fanning fears that the bear market on Wall Street could stifle the U.S. economic recovery, which would hit economies around Asia. \par \par \uc2  "Essentially, this WorldCom news is just further evidence that the U.S. model that we all salivated over is unraveling at a pretty good pace," said Michael Jansen, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at National Australia Bank. \par \par \uc2  Tony Pearson, senior economist at the bank, added, "Fear is growing that no reported U.S. corporate accounts can be trusted." \par \par \uc2  In late Asian screen trade, Nasdaq September stock futures were down about 5% from their U.S. close and S&P 500 stock futures were down nearly 3%, promising heavy losses on Wall Street Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Nikkei stock average tumbled 4.0% to close at 10,074.56, while Seoul's main stock index lost 7.2%, Taiwan sank 3.6% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index fell 2.4%. Malaysia's stock market was the region's strongest, down only 0.5% in late trade as investors welcomed Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad's decision to remain in office until October 2003. \par \par \uc2  There was an immediate surge of safe-haven buying in gold and U.S. Treasury bonds. Spot gold jumped as much as $4.95 per ounce from levels in New York Tuesday, hitting $325.50/oz. Treasurys trading, usually quiet in Asia, saw frenetic buying interest, pushing the yield on the 10-year bond down to 4.74% from 4.82% late in New York. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices were strong in most countries. In South Korea, dealers reported some panic buying as the yield on 3-year government bonds fell 0.25 percentage point to 5.50%. Japanese government bonds posted moderate gains. \par \par \uc2  In the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the U.S. dollar slid about 1% against the yen to a seven-month low of Y120.18, despite yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which traders estimated at around US$4 billion. Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained in line with the yen, and the euro neared parity against the dollar for the first time since early 2000. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts downplayed the long-term impact of the WorldCom scandal. In Tokyo, Shinko Securities strategist Tsuyoshi Segawa said it wouldn't be as devastating as Enron's failure because more Japanese and international investors were exposed to Enron debt. Few Japanese firms and local governments still hold WorldCom bonds, he said. \par \par \uc2  There was talk that Asian equities could eventually benefit from an inflow of funds fleeing the U.S. Thomas Appelkamp, institutional salesman at Dresdner Kleinwort-Wasserstein in Tokyo, said Japanese stocks could now outperform the Dow Jones Industrial Average in coming months. Investors might want to favor domestic demand-related stocks in Tokyo over high-technology, auto and electronics shares that could be vulnerable to U.S. market fallout, he said. \par \par \uc2  But in Asia on Wednesday there seemed little immediate prospect of new capital flows into regional stock markets, given the fear that events in the U.S. could damage Asian economies. Speculation grew that Asian central banks would have to postpone or cancel any plans to lift interest rates in coming months. \par \par \uc2  In Australia, for example, there have been expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will hike rates after its policy-making board meets next Tuesday. But bond dealers now say a rate hike next week is unlikely, and there may only be one more tightening this year; the path for rates in 2003 is unclear. \par \par \uc2  "If we have another week like we have had over the last week, then we would be starting to talk about rates going down here," said Jonathan Hale, senior bond trader at Westpac Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  Among the hardest hit Asian shares Wednesday were telecommunications companies, as the WorldCom news soured sentiment toward the sector generally. In Tokyo, Nippon Telegraph & Telephone Corp. finished at Y477,000, down 3.8%. In the Singapore market, Singapore Telecommunications was down 2.2% at S$1.31 in late trade, after touching a record intra-day low of S$1.29. \par \par \uc2  Shares in exporters were hurt both by worries about the U.S. economy and by the appreciation of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which will undermine Asian firms' competitiveness in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Technology shares were hurt both by WorldCom and by news after Tuesday's U.S. market close that Micron Technology Inc. posted a surprise third-quarter loss of $24.2 million - new evidence of poor demand for personal computers. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, NEC Corp. shares closed 3.2% lower at Y798, while Sony Corp. shares closed at Y5,810, down 4.8%. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said they expected the Asian technology sector to underperform the region's stock markets in coming months, as investors will remain skittish about the sector globally. \par \par \uc2  "WorldCom closes the book on the fairy tale that we are going to get a quick turn in the technology sector tomorrow, next week, next month or even next quarter," said Jeff Matthews, general partner at hedge fund Ram Partners in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Douglas Whitman, fund manager at Whitman Capital in the U.S., said of the WorldCom news, "I think it's a crippling thing. I am very worried about tomorrow." \par \par \uc2  -By Brian Fowler, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4041; brian.fowler@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Helen Ubels and Doug Appell contributed to this story.) \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020020626dy6q000hs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
