\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Slides Against Euro, Yen on News of WorldCom Fraud \b0\par\par\uc2  By Grainne McCarthy \par \uc2 1,002 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2002\par 00:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Whacked by the startling revelation of a major accounting fraud at WorldCom Inc., the dollar tumbled against its key counterparts Wednesday, at one point coming within striking distance of parity with the euro and prompting talk of a major meltdown in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar did claw back some lost ground later in the session in line with gains in U.S. stocks, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still ended New York trade around nine-month lows against the yen, a 28-month trough against the euro and at its weakest mark against the Swiss franc in close to three years. At one point, the euro traded as high as 99.45 U.S. cents, stoking talk that the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could reach the elusive bar of parity with the dollar as early as this week. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the dollar shrugged off a low-key monetary policy statement from the Federal Open Market Committee, after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its key interest rates unchanged for the fourth time this year. The Fed said the economic outlook remains foggy, with risks "balanced" between inflation and too-slow economic growth. The phraseology implied the Fed isn't preparing to raise or lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  As well as being sold off heavily on the WorldCom scandal, market participants also took comments from President George W. Bush as an effective green light to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Signaling that the U.S. won't likely step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and try to prop up the flagging greenback, Mr. Bush said late Tuesday that market forces and economic fundamentals will determine the dollar's exchange rate, in the process casting doubt about the very existence of a so-called strong-dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  Treasury officials scrambled to mop up any confusion sparked by Mr. Bush's rare foray into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, insisting that there was no change to the Bush administration's commitment to a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Nothing new. No change in dollar policy," a Treasury spokesman said. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants viewed Mr. Bush's statement as a sign that the government won't stand in the way of a falling dollar, certainly as long as the decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't transform into a straight-line plunge. \par \par \uc2  "The price of explicitly abandoning [the policy] is very high," said Jason Bonanca, vice president and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York. He said Mr. Bush's remarks were "one of many pieces of commentary that suggest that there is significant dilution of exactly what it means." \par \par \uc2  While the sell-off in the dollar might have been overdone -- on an intraday basis, the euro jumped as much as 1.5%, sterling by 1.4% and the yen by 2% -- the WorldCom fraud nonetheless focused attention on the deep doubts hanging over what had been a covenant of faith between U.S. corporations and shareholders. It also raised the question of how many other U.S. companies might have engaged in similar practices. \par \par \uc2  "This brings attention to speculative excesses that bloated values in the telecommunications sector," said John Lonski, chief economist at Moody's Investors Service. \par \par \uc2  As a result of WorldCom, "investors will be increasingly wary of building up further positions within U.S. equities," which in turn is expected to "speed up the pace of dollar's decline," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York. \par \par \uc2  There were already key signs that the retreat from U.S. assets -- in particular equities -- was gaining pace, which has already been weighing on the dollar. But the prospect of a rapid withdrawal from U.S. asset markets raises an additional raft of fears for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has effectively metamorphosed into a barometer of global risk. \par \par \uc2  "If the dollar's decline turns explosive, this could compound the problems of the U.S. asset markets as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses raise fears of a massive capital flight out of the U.S.," said Mark Cliffe, chief economist at ING Barings. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the euro was at 98.14 cents, well off its intraday high of 99.45 cents, but still stronger than 97.92 cents late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was at 119.90 yet, again off its lows for the day near 118.98 yet, but still weaker than 121.38 yet late Tuesday in New York. The dollar was also at 1.4911 Swiss francs from 1.4990 francs late Tuesday in New York. Sterling was at $1.5267, up from $1.5238 and $1.5068. \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed even to get lasting respite from several rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is keen to stop a strong yen from derailing Japan's frail economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  There were some silver linings for the greenback, however. The dollar gained against many of the key peripheral \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- including the so-called commodity bloc of the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars as well as most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At one point the Mexican peso, which tends to trade in tandem with the dollar, saw its rate against the dollar drop as far as 10.0725 Mexican pesos, a two-year low and well down from late Tuesday's 9.8850 pesos. But the peso also recovered some ground as the dollar clawed back, ending the day around 9.9500 pesos. \par \par \uc2  Analysts reckon there's more pain in store for Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also. "They're going to weaken in regard to the dollar on risk aversion alone," said Greg Anderson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at FleetBoston Financial. "It's a short-term phenomenon because people see chaos and the potential for a crash. The things that drive the dollar drives the Latin \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker." \par \par \uc2  -- John Parry contributed to this report \par \par \uc2  Write to Grainne McCarthy at grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 2002 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020626dy6q006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
