\par\par \b \uc2  Japan says stable yen good for world, not just Japan. \b0\par\par\uc2 668 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2002\par 17:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 25 (Reuters) - Japanese officials, fearful a rapidly rising yen could choke off economic recovery, kept up the pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Tuesday, saying a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not only good for Japan but for the world. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa, whose ministry controls \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday to restore order to trading. \par \par \uc2  "It was necessary for the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stabilise not only for Japan but for the global economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said the government wanted a degree of orderliness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, which, he said, had lost a sense of direction. \par \par \uc2  "If the government had not sent a signal, the market could have gone off in one direction and a lot of people would have been hurt," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Trade Minister Takeo Hiranuma was more specific, saying it would be problematic if the dollar fell sharply below 120 yen. \par \par \uc2  Asked about appropriate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, he said: "I cannot say because firms have strength, (but) it would be difficult (for them) if the dollar fell sharply below 120 yen." \par \par \uc2  The dollar was trading around 121.90 yen at 0135 GMT, up from from its 121.63 yen finish in New York. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday, selling dollars for yen for the first time in three weeks after the yen surged to a seven-month high of 120.80 to the dollar in offshore trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar spiked up briefly to 122.80 yen but failed to maintain the upward momentum due to profit-taking selling. \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimate that Japan has spent about $25 billion over the past few weeks to stem a rise in the yen, which has climbed about 10 percent since April 1 as the U.S. dollar slides against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on pessimism over U.S. financial assets. \par \par \uc2  A reporter suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been very effective. Shiokawa replied: "Yes, but I think the market is taking a wait-and-see stance." \par \par \uc2  Hiranuma said the yen's strength had halted somewhat, but vigilance must be maintained. "There are various effects on industry so we need to watch carefully." \par \par \uc2  The government fears a stronger yen, which makes exports more expensive, will hurt the automakers and big export-oriented industries who pulled Japan from recession in the first quarter when the economy expanded by 1.4 percent from October-December. \par \par \uc2  ONE VOICE \par \par \uc2  Zembei Mizoguchi, head of the finance ministry's international bureau, reinforced Shiokawa's comment on the desirability of stable rates for Japan and the world. \par \par \uc2  "A strong recovery in the Japanese economy is important for the world economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  Top financial diplomat Haruhiko Kuroda, normally the government's chief spokesman on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, said he had nothing to add to Shiokawa's statement on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Shiokawa said he wanted the Bank of Japan, which holds a one-day policy board meeting on Wednesday, to keep yen it sells in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the banking system. \par \par \uc2  Normally, the BOJ, which acts as agent for the government in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , "sterilises" the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds by selling an offsetting amount of government or other debt. \par \par \uc2  "I want them to keep as much funds as possible in the current account deposits" parked at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. \par \par \uc2  In theory, the extra liquidity from unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the effect of keeping downward pressure on interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Money traders said they believe all recent dollar-buying, yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unsterilised. \par \par \uc2  Most economists expect the BOJ's nine-member Policy Board to keep policy unchanged when it meets on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has kept interest rates near to zero for more than a year, and its options are limited. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ currently targets current account deposits parked at the BOJ at 10-15 trillion yen ($82-$123 billion), largely exceeding the amount required in the money market. \par \par \uc2  ($1=121.62 yen). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020625dy6p00tt2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
