\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar takes breather after Wall St rebounds. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Hideyuki Sano \par \uc2 814 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2002\par 08:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, June 25 (Reuters) - The dollar managed to hold steady versus the euro and the yen in Asia on Tuesday after a rebound in U.S. stocks induced profit-taking in a market that had gone short of dollars after its recent slide. But most dealers doubt the dollar's three-month-old fall is coming to an end, given the nagging worries about the U.S. economy and especially about the widening U.S. current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was flat throughout the Asian day. As of 0600 GMT, it was quoted at 121.63/68 yen barely changed from its late New York level around 121.63. \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at 97.12/17 cents little changed from 97.05 in late New York but more than a full cent off its 28-month high of 98.17 hit late in the European day on Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was helped by a turnaround on Wall Street on Monday, when the Dow Jones industrial average closed up 0.30 percent at 9,281.82 after earlier dropping over one percent. \par \par \uc2  "The euro's sharp fall from its high made many think now is the time to take profits," said Toshiyuki Aoyagi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nissho Iwai Financial Link. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was supported by wariness about Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Monday to buy dollars. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar backed off from its high after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but the market was very careful about making further follow-through sales as caution over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains," said Koichi Abe, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  section manager at Aozora Bank. \par \par \uc2  The dollar spiked up briefly to 122.80 yen after Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but then drifted back down near to the 121.10-121.20 yen level where the BOJ had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  QUESTIONS ABOUT \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 'S STANCE \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimate that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sold some 3.0 trillion yen ($24.67 billion) in the market over the past few weeks in order to hold the yen down. \par \par \uc2  However, some are now scenting a slight shift in the Japanese government's stance. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry, which aggressively bought the dollar when it fell below 124 yen last month, did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  until some way below that on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The Finance Ministry seems to have toned down its rhetoric. Recently, I haven't heard them saying they will take decisive action," said Toshiyuki Aoyagi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nissho Iwai Financial Link, referring to a phrase ministers and ministry official have used repeatedly until recently. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa simply said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday to bring some order back to the market. \par \par \uc2  "I think the authorities just want to curb the speed of the yen's rise, rather than to stop it," Aoyagi said. \par \par \uc2  Many dealers suspect the Japanese government was asked to soften its stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Group of Seven finance ministers' meeting earlier this month, even though officials denied \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues were discussed at the meeting. \par \par \uc2  They also think the U.S. is now happy with a weaker dollar, despite its official strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  "It's becoming quite obvious that the U.S. policy is now one of benign neglect, for a weakening of the dollar," said Takehiro Sato, an economist at Morgan Stanley. \par \par \uc2  Thus dealers are keeping an eye on the Group of Seven summit starting from Wednesday for any clues on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  The focus ahead of that is on U.S. consumer confidence data for June due at 1400 GMT on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The figures are forecast to show the sharpest drop since the weeks following the September 11 attacks as a plunging stock market and doubts about the economic recovery are dampening the mood in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Economists polled by Reuters forecast the consumer confidence index would fall to 106.0 in June from 109.8 in May. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bid prices at 0559 GMT. All data taken from Reuters \par \par \uc2  with percent change calculated from the daily U.S. close at 2130 \par \par \uc2  GMT. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                        Last     US Close  % Chg  YTD %   2001 Cls\par                                 24 Jun.\par                        -----------------------------------------\par  Euro/dlr              0.9699   0.9701   -0.02    +8.93   0.8904\par  Dlr/yen               121.63   121.70   -0.06    -7.60   131.63\par  Euro/yen              117.97   118.10   -0.11    +0.62   117.24\par  Dlr/swiss             1.5124   1.5119   +0.03    -8.86   1.6594\par  Stg/dlr               1.5000   1.5010   -0.07    +3.16   1.4541\par  Can/dlr               1.5213   1.5205   +0.05    -4.42   1.5917\par  Aus/dlr               0.5717   0.5699   +0.32   +11.97   0.5106\par  Euro/swiss            1.4674   1.4666   +0.05    -0.72   1.4780\par  Euro/stg              0.6465   0.6460   +0.08    +5.59   0.6123.