\par\par \b \uc2  Jittery Dollar Slips After Consumer Confidence Declines \b0\par\par\uc2  By John Parry and Tyler Lifton \par \uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2002\par 16:45\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The dollar slipped to session lows  against the euro and the Swiss franc Tuesday as a weaker-than-expected key consumer confidence report fed anxieties about the greenback's vulnerabilities to waning growth. \par \par \uc2  The Conference Board's consumer confidence report for June was only mildly softer than expected, falling to 106.4,  compared with the 107.6 consensus estimate and down from an  upwardly revised reading of 110.3 in May. But the downward trend nonetheless spooked dollar investors further. \par \par \uc2  Although other U.S. asset markets hardly budged in response to the data, the dollar's downtick underscored its vulnerability to any indication that the pivotal consumer spending area, which accounts for some two thirds of U.S. economic activity, might be losing momentum. \par \par \uc2  In the wake of the report's release at 10:00 a.m. EDT, the euro reached session highs against the dollar, at around  $0.9744, while the dollar brushed session lows against the Swiss franc at CHF1.5064. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, against the yen, the U.S. unit remained trapped in a fairly narrow range, as traders remained on high alert for the threat of more \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to weaken the yen, as occurred Monday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's decline was to some extent reversed by the somewhat supportive effects of U.S. existing home sales data, also released at 10:00 a.m. EDT, indicating some sectors of consumer spending remain ebullient. \par \par \uc2  Existing home sales fell slightly in May but total sales  hit their fourth-highest level ever in the month, the National Association of Realtors said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar has also been helped by U.S. stock markets extending their gains during recent trade, with the Dow Jones Industrial Average up about 1.3%, but strategists said  that underlying negative sentiment is still encircling the greenback. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday's U.S. data reports "didn't do anything to change the sentiment for the U.S. economy," while "general  bearishness towards the U.S. corporate sector," with worries over companies profits and declining foreign investment flows into the U.S. will continue to weigh on the  dollar, said Rebecca Patterson, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with JP Morgan in New York. \par \par \uc2  -By John Parry, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096; john.parry@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -By Tyler Lifton, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096 \par \par \uc2  tyler.lifton@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1074 1863 28038 4164 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020020625dy6p007k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
