\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-U.S. stocks and dollar rebound, bonds slip. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Chelsea Emery \par \uc2 1,209 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2002\par 00:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 24 (Reuters) - U.S. stocks erased early losses to close modestly higher on Monday as investors snatched up shares from the bargain basement after weeks of selling had driven stocks to near three-year lows, last touched in the weeks after the Sept. 11 attacks. \par \par \uc2  The dollar, which had touched multi-year lows against the euro and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also had erased its losses by the close - helped by Wall Street's rebound and by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of buying dollars and selling yen. \par \par \uc2  Energy prices rose, spurred by tension in the Middle East and the meeting on Wednesday in Vienna of the ministers of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasury prices, which had advanced earlier in the day on flight-to-safety buying, slid as stocks recovered. \par \par \uc2  Gold earlier touched three-and-a-half-week highs, lifted by the renewed dollar weakness, stock market losses and fresh tension in the Middle East, but closed up from its lows as the stock market found its footing. \par \par \uc2  On Wall Street, U.S. market watchers linked the stock rally partly to U.S. President George W. Bush's plan to outline a long-awaited speech on the Middle East, offering a road map to statehood for the Palestinians. \par \par \uc2  "The Bush speech this afternoon added fuel to the fire, but the rally was under way," said Matthew Johnson, managing director of trading at Lehman Brothers. "There were rumors that Microsoft will pre-announce to the upside. But whether it is an oversold market looking for a reason to rally, who knows?" \par \par \uc2  Microsoft Corp. jumped $1.88, or 3.6 percent, to $54.16, while Intel Corp. advanced 60 cents, or 3.2 percent, to $19.33. Both stocks are among the biggest names traded on the Nasdaq and boosted that tech-laden index. They are among the 30 stocks in the blue-chip Dow average and lifted the Dow as well. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones industrial average closed up 28.03 points, or 0.3 percent, at 9,281.82, according to the latest available figures. The Dow had earlier fallen more than 1 percent. The broader Standard & Poor's 500 Index was up 3.58 points, or 0.36 percent, at 992.72. The technology-laced Nasdaq Composite Index was up 19.38 points, or 1.34 percent, at 1,460.34. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve's policy-makers meet Tuesday and Wednesday, but the Street expects them to leave short-term interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day, all three key market gauges fell to fresh 2002 lows, adding to losses from Friday, when the market wrapped up a fifth straight week of declines. \par \par \uc2  Goldman Sachs had added to negative sentiment after it cut its forecasts on computer giant International Business Machines Corp. and software maker Siebel Systems Inc. \par \par \uc2  IBM erased earlier losses to rise 98 cents to $69.73, while Siebel added 52 cents to $13.99, also recouping from earlier declines. \par \par \uc2  WorldCom Inc. lost a quarter of its market value, or 31 cents to 91 cents after Salomon Smith Barney cut its rating on the telecom company's stock, saying spending by large corporations has worsened. \par \par \uc2  Investor concern over the strength of the economic recovery in the United States sent the dollar to a two-year low against the euro, and to fresh lows against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Swiss franc and sterling. \par \par \uc2  In European equities markets, the FTSE Eurotop 300 index lost 2.5 percent as setbacks for highly indebted French companies Vivendi and France Telecom and the insurance sector added to the gloom. In London, the blue-chip FTSE 100 index fell 63.4 points, or 1.38 percent, to end at 4,541.9, its lowest close in nine months. \par \par \uc2  "The near-term outlook remains gloomy: sentiment, liquidity and earnings expectations point to fresh lows," Michael Hartnett, director of European equity strategy at Merrill Lynch, told clients in a note. \par \par \uc2  However, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to curb yen strength helped Tokyo stocks reverse earlier declines. \par \par \uc2  The thoughts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Europe turned to "parity party" champagne as the once-derided euro soared briefly above 98 cents before easing to trade just below this level. During the session, the euro hit a two-year high at 98.16 cents - a gain of 4 cents in four weeks - but still 19 cents, or 16 percent, below its launch level of $1.17 in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  By the close on Monday in New York, the dollar finished the U.S. session virtually unchanged against the euro, which was quoted at 97.05 cents. Against the Japanese yen, the dollar was trading at 121.63 yen at Monday's New York close, up from 121.09 yen late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The big problem for the dollar is the weakness of U.S. asset markets, which means the United States will find it hard to finance their current account deficit," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in London. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, concerned that a soaring yen and weak greenback will hit the countries' exporters and make it still more difficult for the economy to sail out of the doldrums, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The move lifted the dollar by more than a yen in Asian trade after it fell to seven-month lows below 121 yen Friday. \par \par \uc2  In Tokyo, the Nikkei average climbed 1.13 percent, or 116.97 points, to close at 10,471.32. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Treasuries were mostly weaker late on Monday, with a turnaround in equities from eight-month lows. \par \par \uc2  In New York, two year Treasury notes struggled to hold positive ground in afternoon trade, up 1/32 at 100-22/32, yielding 2.88 percent, down slightly from 2.89 percent late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year U.S. Treasury note lost 16/32 to 100-11/32, yielding 4.83 percent. That was up from 4.77 percent late on Friday and off of last Wednesday's 4.72 percent close, the 10-year's lowest closing yield of the year. The 30-year bond slid 27/32 to 98-26/32, yielding 5.46 percent. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold closed in London at $325.60/326.10 a troy ounce, up from $324.40/324.90 at the close of trade in New York on Friday after hitting a session best of $326.70 an ounce. \par \par \uc2  In New York, COMEX gold for August delivery fell 40 cents to $324.70 an ounce, its first loss in a week, after rising as high as $327.70 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  Gold was underpinned by the tension in the Middle East as Israeli forces killed six Palestinians in a helicopter missile strike on a car carrying Islamic militants in the West Bank. \par \par \uc2  On June 4, gold hit $330.30 an ounce, its highest level in more than 2-1/2 years. \par \par \uc2  Crude oil futures leaped amid the Middle East turmoil and before OPEC's output policy meeting on Wednesday. NYMEX August crude gained 65 cents to settle at $26.47 a barrel. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020624dy6o015tn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
