\par\par \b \uc2  Foreigners don't dump U.S. agencies despite dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Lynn Adler \par \uc2 842 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 19:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, June 24 (Reuters) - Overseas investors, who have soaked up U.S. agency debt for higher yields over Treasuries and shelter from stocks and geopolitical risk, are holding on to those securities despite a slumping U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steep descent is forcing some foreign investors to dump dollar-denominated debt, aiming to skirt \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses and higher hedging costs. But agency debt, a top fixed-income performer this year, remains popular for its high quality and returns. \par \par \uc2  But debt sold by U.S. home finance giants Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac and the Federal Home Loan Banks is viewed as safe, predictable and easily traded. With Treasury yields at five-and six-month lows, agencies remain relatively attractive, strategists said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar, yes, is weaker, but there is not a massive unloading of dollar assets," said Astrid Adolfson, economist at MCM MoneyWatch. If anything, "there's more of a slowing of buying than necessarily a real selling" of U.S. agency securities. \par \par \uc2  The dollar slid last week to lows unseen for 26 months against the euro and seven months versus the yen. Tumbling stocks and corporate profit warnings have eroded confidence in the U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  More foreign central banks have been approved to buy U.S. agency debt. More are using the Lehman Aggregate Index as a portfolio model, and agencies represent about 12 percent of that index. \par \par \uc2  Add \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the equation. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling yen and buying dollars, investing that U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in agencies as well as Treasuries, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "For a lot of foreign investors, central banks included, there's no real reason to be holding Treasuries inside of five years when you get better spread and better returns holding agencies and you have essentially the same liquidity," said Beth Hammack, head of the agency desk at Goldman Sachs & Co. \par \par \uc2  "We really haven't seen any slippage," in off-shore buying of agencies. "What we're hearing back (from Asian accounts) and what we've seen from our flows is skepticism that the euro's strength will persist, and that the euro has not become a core part of their holidings in the way dollar-based assets are." \par \par \uc2  HOLDINGS HOLDING \par \par \uc2  Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying of U.S. agencies and Treasuries has been similar this year, based on Federal Reserve data. \par \par \uc2  The Fed held $154 billion of agency debt in custody for foreign accounts in the week ended June 19, up from about $21 billion from the end of 2001. Treasury holdings rose to $619 billion in the most recent week, up $19 billion this year. \par \par \uc2  Last year, agencies holdings in those accounts jumped nearly $31 billion as Treasuries holdings gained $10 billion. \par \par \uc2  "You probably are reaching a maturation point," said Shrikant Ramamurthy, agency strategist at Greenwich Capital. \par \par \uc2  "There were some very large allocations in the last two years from Treasuries into agencies. A few very big central banks moved some of their Treasury holdings into agencies," he said. "There aren't other central banks like that left to make that (sized) allocation from Treasuries to agencies." \par \par \uc2  Ramamurthy doesn't see much holdings erosion, however. \par \par \uc2  "What you're going to see now is that these central banks are going to maintain their allocations," he said, adding "there won't be any big shifts either way." \par \par \uc2  WALLETS CLOSING? \par \par \uc2  Some market players worry that agencies are vulnerable if the dollar stays weak, particularly if Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac start issuing more debt. The mortgage-backed securities the agencies issue and buy have grown expensive this year, so the home funding giants have scaled back the agency debt offerings used to fund such activity. \par \par \uc2  "The sectors that might have to pay for a change in the dollar outlook are probably the two sectors" that have been raking in heavy foreign investment - equities and agencies, said Thaddeus Carlson, portfolio manager in the Treasury and agency sector at J.P. Morgan Fleming Asset Management. \par \par \uc2  "We are a little worried that agencies could cheapen up ... if the foreign participants just hold off a little bit, not buy as much as they have in the past," he said. \par \par \uc2  But even amid concern that foreign investment in agency debt may be stalling, strategists say the larger concern is for other dollar-based sectors. \par \par \uc2  "Where they have been cutting back is in the stock portfolios," said Adolfson. \par \par \uc2  Foreign flows into U.S. corporate bonds are also shrinking, with net purchases down 30.4 percent in the first quarter, based on recent Treasury Department data. \par \par \uc2  "To me, it's not an agencies story as much as a corporates story," Ramamurthy said of the falling dollar's impact on foreign investment in U.S. assets. "Europeans don't buy as many agencies, they buy a lot more corporates." \par \par \uc2  Agencies have returned 4.17 percent this year, more than the 3.925 percent for Treasuries and 3.15 percent for corporates, according to Merrill Lynch. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020624dy6o00yq7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
