\par\par \b \uc2  GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar, stocks drop on U.S. economy woes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Gerrard Raven \par \uc2 934 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2002\par 14:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, June 24 (Reuters) - The dollar and stocks took a further market pounding on Monday as worries that the United States could be in for about another bout of recession spooked already nervous investors. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hit by a further fall in stock prices in New York on Friday, fell to a more than two year low against the euro, and its weakness prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a surge in the yen. \par \par \uc2  The FTSE Eurotop 300 index lost more than 2-1/2 percent at one stage as setbacks for highly-indebted French firms Vivendi and France Telecom and for the insurance sector added to the gloom. \par \par \uc2  However, the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to curb yen strength helped Tokyo stocks to reverse earlier falls. \par \par \uc2  The thoughts of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers in Europe turned to parity party champagne as the once-derided euro soared briefly above $0.98 before easing to trade just below this level. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has now risen four cents in four weeks but is still 19 cents or 16 percent below its launch level of 1.17 in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  The dollar also hit lows of 2-1/2 years against the Swiss franc, whose safe haven status was burnished by news that six Palestinians died in an Israeli helicopter missile strike in the Gaza Strip, and 17 months against sterling. \par \par \uc2  Analysts blamed the dollar's weakness on the fall in U.S. stock prices, which continued on Friday when the Dow Jones industrial average lost 1.89 percent to close at its lowest level since October 31. \par \par \uc2  "The U.S. dollar's being hit really badly. We're seeing sellers all over the place. There is a smell of capitulation in the market," said Tom Hougaard, chief market strategist at London spread betting firm City Index. \par \par \uc2  U.S. stock index futures pointed to a further easing when Wall Street began trading for the week. \par \par \uc2  Middle class Americans heavily invested in shares feel poorer, and therefore reluctant to spend, due to Wall Street's continued weakness which has been unsettled by corporate scandals, an uncertain economic outlook and ongoing tensions in the Middle East. \par \par \uc2  This has provoked fears that the economy could go into reverse again. \par \par \uc2  And economists also worry that the United States will have trouble sucking in desperately needed investment. \par \par \uc2  "The big problem for the dollar is the weakness of U.S. asset markets which means the United States will find it hard to finance their current account deficit," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in London. \par \par \uc2  BANK OF JAPAN STEPS IN \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Japan, concerned that a soaring yen will hit the countries' exporters and make it still more difficult for the economy to sail out of the doldrums, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in three weeks. \par \par \uc2  The move lifted the dollar by more than a yen in Asian trade after it fell to seven-month lows below 121 yen on Friday, but by 1000 GMT, it had eased to trade around 121.37 yen. \par \par \uc2  However, Japanese stocks rallied, with the Nikkei average ending 1.13 percent higher, with investors encouraged that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength seriously. \par \par \uc2  European stocks staged an initial rally in what analysts saw as a technical bounce following heavy selling on Friday linked to the so-called "triple witching" expiry of stock index options and futures. \par \par \uc2  But by 1150 GMT, the FTSE Eurotop 300 index, which on Friday ended at its weakest level since the September 11 attacks on U.S. cities, was down a further 2.4 percent, just off its day's lows. \par \par \uc2  "The near-term outlook remains gloomy: sentiment, liquidity and earnings expectations point to fresh lows," said Michael Hartnett, director of European equity strategy at Merrill Lynch, said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  French shares suffered two blows. Moody's credit ratings agency cut the ratings of France Telecom to just one notch above junk status, sending its shares down 11.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  And a plan by Vivendi to raise its stake in its telecoms unit Cegetel to over 50 percent, enabling it to use the strong cash flow to reduce debt, was spiked when Britain's BT Group Plc said conditions were not ripe for it to sell its stake. \par \par \uc2  Vivendi shares were down more than 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  Many financial and insurance stocks suffered after CGNU said it had used around two billion pounds ($3 billion) of expected earnings from current policies to boost solvency margins. \par \par \uc2  CGNU lost two percent, Zurich Financial 22 percent and ING Groep almost five percent. \par \par \uc2  GERMAN DATA BOOSTS GOVERNMENT BONDS \par \par \uc2  Yields on European government debt dipped after data from German states showed an easing in inflation in June, reducing the prospects of an imminent rise in interest rates from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  AT 1200 GMT, the yield on 10-year euro zone government bonds was 2.8 basis points lower at 4.926 percent. \par \par \uc2  The soft dollar pushed gold, already on a bull run because of tensions in the Middle East and the Asian subcontinent, up further. It was fixed in London at $326.25 an ounce compared with $322.70 on Friday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Oil regained some of the losses it sustained on Friday on reports that Venezuela planned to exceed its OPEC production quotas, and at 1200 GMT, Brent crude for August delivery was up 14 cents to $24.89 a barrel. (Gerrard Raven, Treasury desk, +44-20-8542-4868). \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020624dy6o00m82\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/1995 bis 21/08/2023\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\c